QB.add(6,[{"id":6001,"t":"Perspectives on Pregnancy and Birth","s":"Five perspectives on birth are presented. Which pairing of perspective and its focus is correct?","o":["Biomedical – a medical event; focus on illness, pathology and potential problems","Physiological – a medical event; focus on illness, pathology and potential problems","Psychological – a normal physiological event; minimal intervention where possible","Social – birth and motherhood are fulfilling; focus on satisfaction","Cultural – an experience; focus on satisfaction and fulfilment"],"e":"The five perspectives: <b>physiological</b> (a normal physiological event; woman-centric; minimal intervention where possible), <b>biomedical</b> (a medical event; focus on illness, pathology, potential problems), <b>psychological</b> (an experience; focus on satisfaction and fulfilment), <b>social</b> (the type of childbirth is socially determined, e.g. increased intervention) and <b>cultural</b> (birth/motherhood is fulfilling). The slide concludes that no single view is right or complete, and the biopsychosocial approach recognises this.","w":["Physiological sees birth as a normal event, woman-centric.","That is the physiological perspective.","Fulfilment fits the psychological/cultural views; social is about how childbirth type is socially determined.","“An experience” is the psychological perspective."],"k":"Five perspectives on birth; none is complete alone (biopsychosocial).","rn":[["","What is the biomedical perspective on birth?","What is the biomedical perspective on birth?","A medical event; Focus on illness, pathology; Potential problems"]]},{"id":6002,"t":"Perspectives on Pregnancy and Birth","s":"A midwife sees birth as a normal event, focuses on the woman and avoids intervention. Name this perspective on birth.","e":"Physiological perspective: birth is a normal physiological event, woman-centric, minimal intervention where possible.","k":"Physiological.","a":["physiological","physiological perspective"],"rn":[["","Physiological perspective on birth?","Physiological perspective on birth?","Normal physiological event; Woman-centred, minimal intervention"]]},{"id":6003,"t":"Perspectives on Pregnancy and Birth","s":"Five perspectives on birth exist (physiological, biomedical, psychological, social, cultural). Which conclusion about them is correct?","o":["No single view is right or complete; the biopsychosocial approach recognises this","The biomedical view is the most complete, as it identifies potential problems","The physiological view is the most complete, as it is woman-centric","The psychological view is the most complete, as it focuses on satisfaction","The cultural and social views together replace the medical perspectives"],"e":"“<b>No single view is right or complete – the biopsychosocial approach recognises this.</b>” Each perspective captures part of the picture: physiology, medical risk, personal experience, social determination and cultural meaning.","w":["No single view is complete.","No single view is complete.","No single view is complete.","The point is that no single view is complete."],"k":"Use a biopsychosocial approach to birth.","rn":[["","Which single perspective on birth is right?","Which single perspective on birth is right?","None is complete; The biopsychosocial approach recognises this"]]},{"id":6004,"t":"Perspectives on Pregnancy and Birth","s":"Which statement about the psychological state of a pregnant woman reflects the introduction?","o":["It is dynamic and fluctuates in every trimester, for both parents","It is stable through pregnancy and changes mainly after birth","It fluctuates in every trimester, but only for the mother","It changes mainly in the third trimester as discomfort increases","It is dynamic in the first trimester, then settles for the rest"],"e":"Pregnancy and the initial weeks/months are a time of <b>significant psychological change for both parents</b>. The psychological state of the pregnant woman is <b>dynamic</b> and changes/fluctuates during <b>every trimester</b>.","w":["It changes throughout pregnancy.","Both parents experience change.","It fluctuates in every trimester.","It fluctuates in every trimester."],"k":"Psychological state is dynamic in every trimester; affects both parents.","rn":[["","Is the psychological state of a pregnant woman fixed?","Is the psychological state of a pregnant woman fixed?","No; Dynamic, fluctuates every trimester"]]},{"id":6005,"t":"Influences on Pregnancy","s":"What percentage of UK pregnancies are unplanned (PHE 2018)?","e":"45% of UK pregnancies are unplanned; risk factors include lower educational attainment, younger age, smoking and substance misuse.","k":"45%.","a":["45","45%","45 percent"],"rn":[["","What % of UK pregnancies are unplanned?","What % of UK pregnancies are unplanned?","45% (PHE 2018)"],["","What are the risk factors for unplanned pregnancy?","What are the risk factors for unplanned pregnancy?","Lower educational attainment; Younger age; Smoking and substance misuse"]]},{"id":6006,"t":"Influences on Pregnancy","s":"Which set of factors does PHE (2018) list as risk factors for unplanned pregnancy?","o":["Lower educational attainment, younger age, smoking and substance misuse","Lower educational attainment, older age, smoking and substance misuse","Higher educational attainment, younger age, smoking and substance misuse","Lower educational attainment, younger age, poor partner support and bereavement","Lower socioeconomic status, previous depression and refugee status"],"e":"Risk factors for unplanned pregnancy (PHE 2018): <b>lower educational attainment</b>, <b>younger age</b>, and <b>smoking and substance misuse</b>.","w":["Younger age.","Lower educational attainment.","Those are PND risk factors (Howard 2014).","Those are PND risk factors."],"k":"Unplanned pregnancy risk: lower education, younger age, smoking/substances.","rn":[["","What are the risk factors for unplanned pregnancy?","What are the risk factors for unplanned pregnancy?","Lower educational attainment; Younger age; Smoking and substance misuse"]]},{"id":6007,"t":"Breastfeeding","s":"Socioeconomic disadvantage increases the risk of several outcomes (de Graaf et al., 2013). Which outcome is <b>not</b> one of them?","o":["Higher rates of breastfeeding","Maternal morbidity, physical and psychological","Maternal mortality","Low birth weight","Infant morbidity and mortality"],"e":"Socioeconomic disadvantage increases the risk of <b>maternal morbidity</b> (physical and psychological), <b>maternal mortality</b>, <b>low birth weight</b> and <b>infant morbidity and mortality</b>. In fact <b>lower SES is linked to less breastfeeding</b>, the opposite of the distractor.","w":["On the list.","On the list.","On the list.","On the list."],"k":"SES disadvantage → maternal and infant morbidity/mortality, LBW.","rn":[["","What are the health benefits of breastfeeding for the baby?","What are the health benefits of breastfeeding for the baby?","Lower mortality from infectious disease; Lower rates of asthma; Immunomodulatory qualities of breast milk"]]},{"id":6008,"t":"Influences on Pregnancy","s":"Why are antenatal classes provided?","o":["Provision of information can change health behaviour","Provision of information reduces the need for midwife care","Provision of support in labour shortens labour","Provision of information prevents postnatal depression","Attendance is required before a hospital birth"],"e":"“<b>Provision of information can change health behaviour</b> = basis for antenatal classes.” Knowledge and information are one of the influences on pregnancy outcome in the slide's model.","w":["Not stated.","That is continuous labour support.","Not stated.","Not stated."],"k":"Information changes behaviour → antenatal classes.","rn":[["","What is the basis for antenatal classes?","What is the basis for antenatal classes?","Information can change; Health behaviour"]]},{"id":6009,"t":"Influences on Pregnancy","s":"Which set of influences shapes pregnancy outcome?","o":["Life events/stress, adaptive/maladaptive behaviour, SES and knowledge","Life events/stress, genetics, socioeconomic factors and the baby’s sex","Adaptive or maladaptive behaviour, medical interventions and breastfeeding","Socioeconomic factors, knowledge and information, the partner’s income and the mode of delivery","Life events/stress, attachment style, breastfeeding and knowledge"],"e":"The diagram links <b>life events and stress</b>, <b>adaptive/maladaptive behaviour</b>, <b>socio-economic factors</b> and <b>knowledge/information</b> to pregnancy outcome.","w":["Genetics and sex were not in the model.","Not the model.","Partner’s income and mode of delivery were not in the model.","Not the model."],"k":"Pregnancy outcome: stress, behaviour, SES, knowledge.","rn":[["","Which 2 personal factors influence pregnancy outcome?","Which 2 personal factors influence pregnancy outcome?","Life events and stress; Adaptive / maladaptive behaviour"]]},{"id":6010,"t":"Influences on Pregnancy","s":"Which behavioural factors are associated with foetal/infant mortality, difficult labour, low birth weight and infant morbidity?","o":["Abuse; alcohol and drugs; nutrient deficiencies; excessive or insufficient weight change","Abuse; alcohol and drugs; nutrient deficiencies; excessive weight gain only","Smoking; alcohol and drugs; lack of antenatal classes; insufficient weight gain only","Abuse; caffeine; nutrient excess; excessive or insufficient weight change","Stress; formula feeding; nutrient deficiencies; excessive weight gain only"],"e":"Behavioural influences associated with foetal/infant mortality, difficult labour, LBW and infant morbidity: <b>physical, sexual or emotional abuse</b>; <b>alcohol and other drugs</b>; <b>nutrient deficiencies</b>; and <b>maternal weight change – excessive or insufficient</b>.","w":["Both excessive and insufficient weight change count.","Antenatal classes were not listed as a risk.","Not the listed factors.","Not the listed factors."],"k":"Harmful behavioural influences in pregnancy.","rn":[["","Which 2 nutrition issues are linked to poor outcomes?","Which 2 nutrition issues are linked to poor outcomes?","Nutrient deficiencies; Too much/little weight change"],["","Which 2 harms in pregnancy are linked to poor outcomes?","Which 2 harms in pregnancy are linked to poor outcomes?","Physical, sexual, emotional abuse; Alcohol and other drugs"]]},{"id":6011,"t":"Influences on Pregnancy","s":"By which two routes can maternal stress affect the foetus?","o":["Direct physiological (hormones, BP, placental blood) and indirect psychological (behaviour)","Direct psychological (behaviour) and indirect physiological (hormones, BP, placental blood)","Direct physiological (hormones, BP) and indirect genetic (inherited stress response)","Direct physiological (placental blood) and indirect via the father’s behaviour","Direct physiological (hormones) and indirect via breastfeeding after birth"],"e":"Stress has a <b>direct physiological response</b> in the mother (<b>hormonal changes, BP</b>) affecting the foetus (e.g. via <b>placental blood supply</b>), and an <b>indirect psychological response</b> through the <b>mother's behaviour</b>, which also affects the foetus.","w":["Reversed.","Not genetic.","The indirect route is the mother’s behaviour.","Not breastfeeding."],"k":"Stress → foetus: direct (hormones/BP/placenta) and indirect (behaviour).","rn":[["","What does the direct physiological stress route involve?","What does the direct physiological stress route involve?","Hormones; BP (blood pressure); Placental blood supply"],["","What are the 2 routes by which stress affects the foetus?","What are the 2 routes by which stress affects the foetus?","Direct physiological; Indirect psychological"]]},{"id":6012,"t":"Influences on Pregnancy","s":"A stressed pregnant woman starts smoking again to cope. Is this a direct or an indirect pathway by which stress affects the foetus?","e":"Stress acts directly (physiological: hormones, BP, placental blood supply) or indirectly (psychological response → mother’s behaviour, e.g. smoking).","k":"Indirect (behavioural).","a":["indirect","indirect pathway","indirect psychological"],"rn":[["","What are the 2 routes by which stress affects the foetus?","What are the 2 routes by which stress affects the foetus?","Direct physiological; Indirect psychological"]]},{"id":6013,"t":"Emotions in Each Trimester","s":"In which trimester are anxiety and depression least common (Lee 2007)?","e":"Mental health problems occur less commonly in the second trimester than in the first and third (reduced nausea, adaptation, information from professionals).","k":"Second trimester.","a":["second","second trimester","2nd","2nd trimester","2"],"rn":[["","In which trimester are anxiety and depression least common?","In which trimester are anxiety and depression least common?","Second (Lee 2007)"]]},{"id":6014,"t":"Emotions in Each Trimester","s":"Why might negative feelings lessen in the second trimester?","o":["Less nausea, more adaptation and some idea of pregnancy care","Less nausea, the end of hormonal changes and more family support","More adaptation, but increasing pelvic girdle pain and insomnia","More information, but returning nausea and tiredness","Less nausea, because most pregnancies are planned"],"e":"In the second trimester mood fluctuations continue but negative feelings may lessen because of <b>reduced nausea/vomiting</b>, <b>more adaptation to changes</b>, and <b>some idea about the pregnancy care</b> from healthcare professionals.","w":["Hormones still change.","Pelvic pain is the third trimester.","Nausea reduces.","Not stated."],"k":"Second trimester: less nausea, adaptation, care information.","rn":[["","Why do negative feelings lessen in the second trimester?","Why do negative feelings lessen in the second trimester?","Less nausea and vomiting; More adaptation; Some idea about pregnancy care"]]},{"id":6015,"t":"Emotions in Each Trimester","s":"A woman in her first trimester swings between excitement and worry and tearfulness. Which factors can influence these fluctuations?","o":["Ailments, planning, finances, family support and loss of independence","Ailments, finances, pelvic girdle pain, insomnia and exhaustion","Planning, finances, attachment style, breastfeeding intention and the baby’s sex","Ailments, family support, reduced nausea and information from professionals","Finances, lifestyle restriction, separation anxiety and baby blues"],"e":"First trimester: fluctuations between positive (excitement, happiness, joy) and negative feelings (disbelief, anticipation, worry, tearfulness), depending on <b>pregnancy ailments</b> (nausea/vomiting, reflux, insomnia), <b>planned/unplanned pregnancy</b>, <b>financial situation</b>, <b>family support</b>, a perception of <b>lifestyle restriction</b> and a sense of <b>loss of independence</b>.","w":["Pelvic pain/insomnia are third trimester.","Not listed.","Reduced nausea/information are second trimester.","Not listed."],"k":"First-trimester mood depends on ailments, planning, finances, support, restriction.","rn":[["","Which life factors affect first-trimester emotions?","Which life factors affect first-trimester emotions?","Planned or unplanned; Finances; Family support"]]},{"id":6016,"t":"Emotions in Each Trimester","s":"Why might negative emotions return in the third trimester?","o":["Discomfort (pelvic girdle pain, backache), insomnia, exhaustion","Increasing nausea and vomiting, insomnia and exhaustion","Increasing discomfort, plus less information and contact from health professionals","Reduced family support, plus loss of independence","Increasing discomfort, plus the onset of baby blues"],"e":"In the third trimester negative feelings may come back because of <b>increasing discomfort</b> (pelvic girdle pain, backache), <b>insomnia</b> and <b>tiredness/exhaustion</b>.","w":["Nausea reduces after the first trimester.","Not stated.","Those are first-trimester factors.","Baby blues are postnatal."],"k":"Third trimester: discomfort, insomnia, exhaustion.","rn":[["","What discomfort occurs in the third trimester?","What discomfort occurs in the third trimester?","Pelvic girdle pain; Backache"]]},{"id":6017,"t":"Support in Pregnancy and Labour","s":"According to Hodnett et al. (2013), which benefit is associated with support in pregnancy and birth (rather than specifically with continuous support in labour)?","o":["Lower likelihood of PND","Shorter labour","Better Apgar scores","Less surgical intervention in labour","Less medical intervention"],"e":"Support in pregnancy and birth: <b>greater perceived control</b>, <b>less stress</b>, <b>greater satisfaction with birth</b>, <b>lower likelihood of traumatic birth</b> and <b>lower likelihood of postnatal depression</b>. <b>Continuous support in labour</b> specifically: <b>shorter labour</b>, <b>less medical/surgical intervention</b> and <b>better Apgar scores</b>.","w":["Continuous labour support.","Continuous labour support.","Continuous labour support.","Continuous labour support."],"k":"Support in pregnancy vs continuous support in labour: different benefit lists."},{"id":6018,"t":"Support in Pregnancy and Labour","s":"A hospital introduces a continuous labour companion for every woman. Which outcomes would be expected?","o":["Shorter labour, less intervention and better Apgar scores","Shorter labour, less intervention and lower rates of baby blues","Longer labour, less intervention and better Apgar scores","Shorter labour, more intervention and better Apgar scores","Shorter labour, less intervention and higher breastfeeding rates"],"e":"<b>Continuous support in labour</b> (Hodnett et al., 2013) is associated with <b>shorter labour</b>, <b>less medical/surgical intervention</b> and <b>better Apgar scores</b>.","w":["Baby blues were not listed.","Shorter labour.","Less intervention.","Breastfeeding was not listed."],"k":"Continuous labour support benefits.","rn":[["","What are the benefits of continuous support in labour?","What are the benefits of continuous support in labour?","Shorter labour; Less medical / surgical intervention; Better Apgar scores"]]},{"id":6019,"t":"Support in Pregnancy and Labour","s":"Which of the following is listed as a possible source of support for pregnant women and new mothers?","o":["Support groups such as NCT, health visitors, midwives and counselling","Obstetric consultants, employers, social media and the GP","Health visitors, midwives, employers and social media groups","Family, friends, social media influencers and online forums","Obstetric consultants and specialist perinatal psychiatrists only"],"e":"Sources of support shown: <b>family</b>, <b>friends</b>, <b>GP</b>, <b>midwife</b>, <b>health visitor</b>, <b>support groups</b> (e.g. <b>NCT</b> – National Childbirth Trust) and <b>counselling</b>.","w":["Employers and social media were not listed.","Not listed.","Not listed.","Not listed."],"k":"Support: family, friends, GP, midwife, HV, NCT, counselling.","rn":[["","Who else supports pregnant women and new mothers?","Who else supports pregnant women and new mothers?","Family and friends; Support groups (e.g. NCT); Counselling"]]},{"id":6020,"t":"Mental Health in Pregnancy","s":"Is pregnancy protective against mental illness?","o":["No – poor mental health in pregnancy predicts postnatal illness","No – but antenatal illness rarely continues after birth","Yes – pregnancy hormones protect most women against mental illness","Yes – but only during the second trimester","Yes – for first pregnancies, but not later ones"],"e":"Quiz question 3 was <b>false</b>: <b>pregnancy is not protective against mental illness</b> (McAllister-Williams et al. 2017). <b>Poor mental health in pregnancy is a strong predictor of mental illness postnatally</b>.","w":["It strongly predicts postnatal illness.","Not protective.","Not protective.","Not protective."],"k":"Pregnancy is not protective; antenatal illness predicts postnatal illness.","rn":[["","What is a strong predictor of postnatal mental illness?","What is a strong predictor of postnatal mental illness?","Poor mental health in pregnancy"]]},{"id":6021,"t":"Mental Health in Pregnancy","s":"Untreated depression in pregnancy is associated with which outcomes?","o":["LBW, preterm delivery, and later emotional problems, ADHD and conduct disorder","LBW, post-term delivery, and later emotional problems, ADHD and conduct disorder","High birth weight, preterm delivery, and later ADHD and conduct disorder","LBW, preterm delivery, and later asthma and infectious disease","LBW, preterm delivery, but no later effects on the child"],"e":"Untreated depression is associated with <b>low birth weight</b> and <b>increased risk of preterm delivery</b>, and adverse outcomes for the child: <b>emotional problems, ADHD, conduct disorder</b>. So quiz question 4 was <b>true</b>.","w":["Preterm.","Low birth weight.","Asthma/infection relate to breastfeeding.","There are later effects."],"k":"Untreated antenatal depression → LBW, preterm, child ADHD/conduct disorder.","rn":[["","Untreated depression in pregnancy: adverse outcomes for the child?","Untreated depression in pregnancy: adverse outcomes for the child?","Emotional problems; ADHD; Conduct disorder"]]},{"id":6022,"t":"Mental Health in Pregnancy","s":"Why might women with mental illness in pregnancy have additional risks to the foetus beyond the illness itself?","o":["They are more likely to misuse alcohol and nicotine","They are more likely to have planned pregnancies","They are less likely to attend antenatal classes and receive information","They are less likely to have nutrient deficiencies","They are more likely to have excessive weight gain"],"e":"Women with mental illnesses are <b>more likely to misuse alcohol, nicotine, etc.</b>, which are behavioural influences linked to adverse foetal and infant outcomes.","w":["Not stated.","Not stated.","Not stated.","Not stated."],"k":"Mental illness ↔ substance misuse in pregnancy.","rn":[["","Women with mental illness in pregnancy are more likely to do what?","Women with mental illness in pregnancy are more likely to do what?","Misuse alcohol, nicotine, etc."]]},{"id":6023,"t":"Baby Blues and Postnatal Depression","s":"Through which main pathway does postnatal depression affect child development, according to Murray et al. (2014)?","o":["Mother–baby interaction: depressed mothers are less sensitive to emotions","Breast milk: depressed mothers produce milk with fewer nutrients","Genetics: depression is inherited directly by the child","Placental blood supply: stress hormones reach the baby after birth","Fathers: depressed mothers reduce the father’s involvement"],"e":"An <b>important pathway is via mother–baby interaction</b> (Murray et al. 2014): <b>depressed mothers are less sensitive to the child's emotions</b>, infants are <b>less responsive to faces and voices</b>, and <b>attachment problems</b> are more likely, with poorer infant learning and cognitive development.","w":["Not stated.","Not the pathway given.","Placental supply is antenatal.","Not stated."],"k":"PND → poorer mother–baby interaction → poorer development.","rn":[["","How does PND affect child development?","How does PND affect child development?","Via mother-baby interaction"],["","How are mothers with PND affected?","How are mothers with PND affected?","Less sensitive to child's emotions"]]},{"id":6024,"t":"Baby Blues and Postnatal Depression","s":"An infant of a depressed mother is assessed. Which finding is most likely?","o":["The infant is less responsive to faces and voices","The infant is more responsive to faces and voices","The infant shows earlier separation anxiety","The infant shows stronger secure attachment","The infant shows faster cognitive development"],"e":"Infants with depressed mothers are <b>less responsive to faces and voices</b>, and problems with psychological attachment are more likely.","w":["Less responsive.","Not stated.","Attachment problems are more likely.","Poorer cognitive development."],"k":"Infants of depressed mothers: less responsive to faces/voices.","rn":[["","How are infants of mothers with PND affected?","How are infants of mothers with PND affected?","Less responsive to faces, voices; Attachment problems more likely"]]},{"id":6025,"t":"Mental Health in Pregnancy","s":"About what fraction of women show PTSD symptoms after vaginal delivery (Olde et al. 2006)?","e":"Childbirth is emotionally intense: PTSD symptoms in about a quarter of women after vaginal deliveries.","k":"About a quarter.","a":["quarter","a quarter","one quarter","1/4","25%","25","one in four","1 in 4"]},{"id":6026,"t":"Baby Blues and Postnatal Depression","s":"On day 4 after birth a mother is tearful and irritable, and by day 9 she feels better. Name the condition.","e":"Baby blues: about half of women, from day 3–4, usually stops by day 10, no treatment necessary.","k":"Baby blues.","a":["baby blues","the baby blues","blues"],"rn":[["","When do baby blues usually stop?","When do baby blues usually stop?","By day 10 postpartum"],["","When do baby blues start?","When do baby blues start?","Within the first week; Usually day 3 or 4"],["","Do baby blues need treatment?","Do baby blues need treatment?","No"]]},{"id":6027,"t":"Baby Blues and Postnatal Depression","s":"A friend tells a new mother that “baby blues usually starts 6–8 weeks after delivery”. Why is this wrong?","o":["It usually starts on day 3–4 and stops by day 10","It usually starts on day 3–4 and lasts about 6 weeks","It usually starts in the third trimester","It usually starts at 2 weeks and lasts a month","It usually starts on day 10 and stops by 6 weeks"],"e":"Baby blues occur within the <b>first week</b>, usually from <b>day 3 or 4</b>, and usually stop by <b>day 10</b> post partum (Henshaw 2003).","w":["It stops by day 10.","It is postnatal.","It starts day 3–4.","It starts day 3–4."],"k":"Baby blues timing: day 3–4 → day 10.","rn":[["","When do baby blues usually stop?","When do baby blues usually stop?","By day 10 postpartum"],["","When do baby blues start?","When do baby blues start?","Within the first week; Usually day 3 or 4"]]},{"id":6028,"t":"Baby Blues and Postnatal Depression","s":"A mother’s low mood after birth has lasted 3 weeks. What should happen?","o":["Screen for depression: this is not baby blues","Reassure: baby blues can last up to 6 weeks","Reassure: baby blues usually stops by 6 months","Screen for PTSD: low mood persisting after 2 weeks suggests birth trauma","Wait until 6 weeks before any assessment"],"e":"Baby blues usually stops by day 10 with no treatment. <b>If low mood lasts more than 2 weeks, screening for depression is warranted.</b>","w":["Baby blues stops by day 10.","It stops by day 10.","Screening for depression is advised.","Screen after 2 weeks."],"k":"Low mood >2 weeks → screen for depression.","rn":[["","When should you screen for depression after birth?","When should you screen for depression after birth?","Low mood over 2 weeks"]]},{"id":6029,"t":"Baby Blues and Postnatal Depression","s":"A mother develops low mood 6 weeks after birth that persists for months; it began in pregnancy. Name the condition (affects 10–15% of mothers).","e":"Postnatal depression: 10–15% of mothers, onset usually within 1–2 months; a third began in pregnancy; increased risk of later depression; not only biological mothers.","k":"PND.","a":["postnatal depression","post natal depression","pnd","postpartum depression","post-natal depression"],"rn":[["","PND","PND","Postnatal Depression"],["","What % of mothers get postnatal depression?","What % of mothers get postnatal depression?","10-15% (Woody 2017)"]]},{"id":6030,"t":"Baby Blues and Postnatal Depression","s":"Which woman has the most risk factors for PND, according to Howard (2014)?","o":["Previous depression, anxiety in pregnancy, poor partner support","No mental health history, strong family support, planned pregnancy","Previous depression, strong partner support, planned pregnancy","Anxiety in pregnancy, exclusive breastfeeding and strong support from family","No mental health history, attended antenatal classes, good finances"],"e":"Risk factors for PND: <b>previous mental health problems</b>, <b>depression or anxiety during pregnancy</b>, <b>poor support</b> from partner, family or friends or marital difficulties, a <b>recent stressful event</b> (e.g. bereavement, relationship ending, job loss), <b>domestic violence or previous abuse</b>, and arriving in a developed country as a <b>refugee or asylum seeker</b>.","w":["No risk factors.","Only one risk factor.","Only one risk factor.","No risk factors."],"k":"PND risk factors (Howard 2014).","rn":[["","Which 2 mental health histories raise PND risk?","Which 2 mental health histories raise PND risk?","Previous mental health problems; Depression/anxiety in pregnancy"],["","Which 2 relationship factors raise PND risk?","Which 2 relationship factors raise PND risk?","Poor support or relationship difficulties; Domestic violence or previous abuse"]]},{"id":6031,"t":"Breastfeeding","s":"Which of the following is <b>not</b> listed as a risk factor for PND?","o":["Exclusive breastfeeding","Experience of domestic violence or previous abuse","Arriving in a developed country as a refugee or to seek asylum","Marital difficulties","Losing a job recently"],"e":"Listed risk factors include previous mental illness, depression/anxiety in pregnancy, poor support/marital difficulties, a recent stressful event (e.g. losing a job), domestic violence/abuse, and refugee/asylum status. Breastfeeding is not a listed risk factor.","w":["Listed.","Listed.","Listed (poor support/marital difficulties).","Listed as a recent stressful event."],"k":"Know the PND risk list.","rn":[["","What does the WHO advise on breastfeeding?","What does the WHO advise on breastfeeding?","Exclusive breastfeeding; First 6 months"],["","UK breastfeeding figures","UK breastfeeding figures","~20% no breastfeeding; <20% exclusive for 6+ months"]]},{"id":6032,"t":"Baby Blues and Postnatal Depression","s":"A father becomes depressed after his baby's birth. Which statement is correct?","o":["PND does not only affect biological mothers","PND only affects mothers, so this cannot be PND","Fathers cannot be affected by childbirth","This must be baby blues","This is tokophobia"],"e":"The slide states PND affects <b>not only (biological) mothers</b>. Becoming a parent is a time of significant psychological change for <b>both parents</b>.","w":["Not only mothers.","Both parents change.","Baby blues is described in mothers in week 1.","Fear of childbirth."],"k":"PND is not limited to biological mothers.","rn":[["","Does PND only affect biological mothers?","Does PND only affect biological mothers?","No"]]},{"id":6033,"t":"Anxiety in Pregnancy","s":"What percentage of women experience anxiety in pregnancy (NICE 2014)?","e":"13% experience anxiety in pregnancy; many experience both anxiety and depression.","k":"13%.","a":["13","13%","13 percent"],"rn":[["","What % of women experience anxiety in pregnancy?","What % of women experience anxiety in pregnancy?","13% (NICE 2014)"]]},{"id":6034,"t":"Anxiety in Pregnancy","s":"What proportion of women are affected by depression and anxiety in the first year after childbirth?","o":["15–20%","1–2%","45%","75%","About half"],"e":"<b>Depression and anxiety also affect 15–20% of women in the first year after childbirth.</b>","w":["Too low.","Unplanned pregnancies.","Not stated.","Baby blues."],"k":"Postnatal year: 15–20% depression/anxiety.","rn":[["","What % of women have depression and anxiety in the first year after birth?","What % of women have depression and anxiety in the first year after birth?","15-20%"]]},{"id":6035,"t":"Anxiety in Pregnancy","s":"Name the anxiety disorder that is an extreme fear of childbirth.","e":"Tokophobia is an extreme fear of childbirth; it can occur alone or alongside depression, like panic disorder, GAD, OCD and PTSD.","k":"Tokophobia.","a":["tokophobia"],"rn":[["","Tokophobia","Tokophobia","Extreme fear of childbirth"],["","Which 3 anxiety disorders occur in pregnancy and postnatally?","Which 3 anxiety disorders occur in pregnancy and postnatally?","Panic disorder; Generalised anxiety disorder; Tokophobia"]]},{"id":6036,"t":"Anxiety in Pregnancy","s":"Which list contains only anxiety disorders that can occur in pregnancy and the postnatal period?","o":["Panic disorder, GAD, OCD, PTSD and tokophobia","Panic disorder, GAD, OCD, PND and tokophobia","Panic disorder, GAD, baby blues, PTSD and tokophobia","Panic disorder, ADHD, OCD, PTSD and tokophobia","Panic disorder, GAD, OCD, PTSD and conduct disorder"],"e":"Perinatal anxiety disorders: <b>panic disorder</b>, <b>generalised anxiety disorder (GAD)</b>, <b>obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD)</b>, <b>post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD)</b> and <b>tokophobia</b>; they can occur alone or coexist with depression.","w":["PND is depression.","Baby blues are not an anxiety disorder.","ADHD is a child outcome.","Conduct disorder is a child outcome."],"k":"Perinatal anxiety disorders list.","rn":[["","Which 2 more anxiety disorders occur in pregnancy?","Which 2 more anxiety disorders occur in pregnancy?","OCD; PTSD"],["","Which 3 anxiety disorders occur in pregnancy and postnatally?","Which 3 anxiety disorders occur in pregnancy and postnatally?","Panic disorder; Generalised anxiety disorder; Tokophobia"]]},{"id":6037,"t":"Attachment","s":"How is attachment defined?","o":["A long-enduring emotional tie to a specific person, sought from birth","A long-enduring emotional tie to any caring adult, sought from 7 months","A short-term emotional tie to the mother, sought from birth","A long-enduring emotional tie to a specific person, formed only after 2 years","A preference for the mother’s smell and voice, present from birth"],"e":"Attachment is a <b>long-enduring emotional tie to a specific person</b>; <b>infants seek attachments from birth to ensure protection</b>.","w":["A specific person, from birth.","Long-enduring, not just the mother.","Infants seek attachments from birth.","Those are bonding routes."],"k":"Attachment: enduring emotional tie, sought for protection.","rn":[["","Attachment","Attachment","Long-enduring emotional tie; To a specific person"]]},{"id":6038,"t":"Attachment","s":"Put the stages of attachment in order.","o":["Pre-attachment → in the making → clear-cut → reciprocal","Pre-attachment → clear-cut → in the making → reciprocal","In the making → pre-attachment → clear-cut → reciprocal","Pre-attachment → in the making → reciprocal → clear-cut","Clear-cut → pre-attachment → in the making → reciprocal"],"e":"Stages: <b>pre-attachment (0–6 wk)</b> → <b>attachment in the making (6 wk–7 mo)</b> → <b>clear-cut attachment (7 mo–2 yr)</b> → <b>reciprocal relationship (2 yr–)</b>.","w":["In the making comes before clear-cut.","Pre-attachment is first.","Reciprocal is last.","Pre-attachment is first."],"k":"Attachment stages in order.","rn":[["","What are the last 2 stages of attachment?","What are the last 2 stages of attachment?","Clear-cut attachment; Reciprocal relationship"],["","What are the first 2 stages of attachment?","What are the first 2 stages of attachment?","Pre-attachment; Attachment in the making"],["","What ages are clear-cut attachment and reciprocal relationship?","What ages are clear-cut attachment and reciprocal relationship?","Clear-cut: 7 months-2 years; Reciprocal: 2 years +"]]},{"id":6039,"t":"Attachment","s":"A 4-month-old prefers the attention and comfort of her parents but does not yet show separation anxiety. Which attachment stage is she in?","o":["Attachment in the making (6 weeks–7 months)","Pre-attachment (0–6 weeks)","Clear-cut attachment (7 months–2 years)","Reciprocal relationship (2 years onwards)","Attachment in the making (0–6 weeks)"],"e":"<b>Attachment in the making (6 wk–7 mo)</b>: a <b>developing preference</b> for the attention and comfort of certain people (especially parents). Separation anxiety appears in <b>clear-cut attachment (7 mo–2 yr)</b>.","w":["She shows a preference.","No separation anxiety yet.","Too young.","Wrong age range."],"k":"6 wk–7 mo: developing preference, no separation anxiety yet.","rn":[["","Attachment in the making: age and feature?","Attachment in the making: age and feature?","6 weeks - 7 months; Developing preference for certain people"],["","What ages are pre-attachment and attachment in the making?","What ages are pre-attachment and attachment in the making?","Pre-attachment: 0-6 weeks; Making: 6 weeks-7 months"]]},{"id":6040,"t":"Attachment","s":"A parent is worried because their 3-month-old cries but settles with anyone. They expected strong separation anxiety by now. Why is this expectation wrong?","o":["Separation anxiety appears in clear-cut attachment, from about 7 months","Separation anxiety appears in attachment in the making, from 6 weeks","Separation anxiety appears in the reciprocal stage, from 2 years","Separation anxiety appears in pre-attachment, from birth","Separation anxiety diminishes after 7 months"],"e":"Separation anxiety belongs to <b>clear-cut attachment (7 mo–2 yr)</b>, when attachment behaviours are directed to specific people. At 3 months the baby is in <b>attachment in the making</b>.","w":["That stage is a developing preference.","It diminishes after 2 years.","Not at birth.","It appears at 7 months."],"k":"Separation anxiety from ~7 months.","rn":[["","When does separation anxiety appear?","When does separation anxiety appear?","Clear-cut attachment stage; 7 months - 2 years"]]},{"id":6041,"t":"Attachment","s":"In the reciprocal relationship stage (from 2 years), what happens to separation anxiety? (one word)","e":"In the reciprocal relationship stage separation anxiety diminishes.","k":"It diminishes.","a":["diminishes","decreases","reduces","lessens","declines","falls","diminish"],"rn":[["","What are the features of the reciprocal relationship stage?","What are the features of the reciprocal relationship stage?","Separation anxiety diminishes; Better at keeping contact; With preferred people"]]},{"id":6042,"t":"Attachment","s":"Which feelings does secure attachment lead to?","o":["Others will be available when needed, and we are worthy of love","Others will be available when needed, but we must rely on ourselves","We are competent, but relationships are unreliable","We are worthy of love only from the mother","Strangers are a threat and must be avoided"],"e":"Secure attachment leads to feelings that <b>others will be available to us in times of need</b> and that <b>we are competent and worthy of love and care</b>.","w":["Secure attachment does not lead to self-reliance only.","Not stated.","Not only the mother.","Not stated."],"k":"Secure attachment: others are available; I am worthy.","rn":[["","Secure attachment leads to which 2 beliefs?","Secure attachment leads to which 2 beliefs?","Others available when needed; We are competent and worthy"]]},{"id":6043,"t":"Attachment","s":"Quality of attachment affects the development of the temporal lobes and which emotional brain system?","e":"Quality of attachment affects neurological development: the limbic system (emotional brain) and temporal lobes (language).","k":"Limbic system.","a":["limbic system","limbic","the limbic system"],"rn":[["","Which brain areas does attachment quality affect?","Which brain areas does attachment quality affect?","Limbic system (emotional brain); Temporal lobes (language)"]]},{"id":6044,"t":"Parent-Infant Bonding","s":"Which of the following is <b>not</b> one of the sensory routes to early parent–infant bonding described?","o":["Taste of formula milk","Physical contact","Smell","Sight","Sound"],"e":"Bonding routes: <b>physical contact</b> (close contact; slings – touch, warmth, sound, smell), <b>smell</b> (mother's pheromones linked with comfort, pleasure, food), <b>sight</b> (3-day-olds distinguish mother visually) and <b>sound</b> (preference for mother's voice).","w":["A bonding route.","A bonding route.","A bonding route.","A bonding route."],"k":"Bonding: contact, smell, sight, sound."},{"id":6045,"t":"Parent-Infant Bonding","s":"From what age (in days) can babies visually distinguish their mother from others?","e":"Sight: 3-day-olds can visually distinguish their mother from others.","k":"3 days.","a":["3","3 days","three days","three","3 day","3 days old"]},{"id":6046,"t":"Parent-Infant Bonding","s":"What is known about mothers who make more early eye contact with their babies?","o":["More sympathetic and more likely to be “authoritative” parents","More sympathetic but more likely to develop PND","Less sympathetic but more likely to breastfeed","More likely to be “authoritarian” parents","More likely to have babies with early separation anxiety"],"e":"Mothers who make more early eye contact are <b>more sympathetic</b> and more likely to be <b>“authoritative” parents</b>.","w":["Not stated.","Not stated.","Authoritative.","Not stated."],"k":"Early eye contact → sympathetic, authoritative parenting.","rn":[["","What are mothers who make more early eye contact like?","What are mothers who make more early eye contact like?","More sympathetic; More likely authoritative parents"]]},{"id":6047,"t":"Parent-Infant Bonding","s":"Why are slings described as useful for bonding?","o":["They keep the baby close: touch, warmth, sound and smell","They let the baby see the mother’s face at all times","They reduce the need for breastfeeding","They help the baby sleep away from the mother","They prevent separation anxiety at 7 months"],"e":"Mothers are encouraged to keep babies in <b>close contact</b>; <b>slings</b> can be useful – <b>touch, warmth, sound, smell</b>.","w":["Not the reason.","Not stated.","Not stated.","Not stated."],"k":"Slings support bonding via the senses.","rn":[["","Physical contact in bonding: what helps?","Physical contact in bonding: what helps?","Keeping babies in close contact; Slings (touch, warmth, sound, smell)"]]},{"id":6048,"t":"Parent-Infant Bonding","s":"Babies quickly associate their mother's smell with what?","o":["Comfort, pleasure and food","Comfort and sleep only","Food and warmth only","Safety from strangers","Pleasure and the father’s presence"],"e":"Babies quickly learn to associate their <b>mother's smell (pheromones)</b> with <b>comfort, pleasure, food</b>.","w":["Also pleasure and food.","Also comfort and pleasure.","Not stated.","Not stated."],"k":"Mother's smell = comfort, pleasure, food.","rn":[["","Smell in bonding: what do babies learn?","Smell in bonding: what do babies learn?","Link mother's smell (pheromones); With comfort, pleasure, food"]]},{"id":6049,"t":"Parent-Infant Bonding","s":"A premature baby spends weeks in an incubator. What is true about bonding in this situation?","o":["All is not lost: kangaroo care helps, and earlier intervention is better","Bonding is permanently impaired, though kangaroo care helps a little","All is not lost, but only the father can bond in this situation","All is not lost, but intervention is best delayed until after 2 years","Bonding is unaffected by incubator care, so no action is needed"],"e":"<b>All is not lost</b> if there is less contact: e.g. children in incubators (advantages of <b>kangaroo care</b>, Kambarami 1998) and adopted children. <b>Children have some resilience</b> (Rutter 2008), and outcomes are <b>better with earlier intervention</b>.","w":["All is not lost.","Not stated.","Earlier intervention gives better outcomes.","Contact matters."],"k":"Reduced early contact can be offset; resilience; early intervention.","rn":[["","What does Rutter (2008) say about children?","What does Rutter (2008) say about children?","Some resilience; Better with earlier intervention"],["","What helps incubator babies bond?","What helps incubator babies bond?","Kangaroo care (Kambarami 1998)"]]},{"id":6050,"t":"Parent-Infant Bonding","s":"A father worries that because he is not breastfeeding, his baby can only form a secure attachment with the mother. What is the best response?","o":["False – attachment with the father is important too","False – babies attach equally to any adult carer","True – only mothers can provide early comfort","True – fathers bond only after 2 years","False – babies do not form any attachments until about 7 months of age"],"e":"The slide states that <b>bonding and attachment with the father is important too</b>, so the statement is false. Attachment is a tie to a specific person, not only the mother.","w":["Attachment is to specific people.","Not stated.","Not stated.","Infants seek attachments from birth."],"k":"Infants can attach to fathers too.","rn":[["","Can babies only be securely attached to mothers?","Can babies only be securely attached to mothers?","No; Father bonding matters too"]]},{"id":6051,"t":"Adaptation to Parenthood","s":"What is the most common anxiety when adapting to parenthood?","o":["Fear of parenthood: will I be a good parent and cope?","Fear of the birth itself and of pain in labour","Fear of breastfeeding difficulties","Fear of loss of independence and lifestyle restriction","Fear of the baby’s health in the first year"],"e":"Most parents have mixed feelings; the <b>most common anxiety is simply fear of parenthood</b>: will I be a good parent? Will I cope? How will my child cope if I do a poor job?","w":["Not the most common.","Not stated.","A first-trimester factor.","Not stated."],"k":"Most common anxiety: fear of parenthood.","rn":[["","What are the first 2 worries in fear of parenthood?","What are the first 2 worries in fear of parenthood?","Am I a good parent?; Will I cope?"]]},{"id":6052,"t":"Adaptation to Parenthood","s":"Silva and Carneiro (2014) describe the birth of a child, particularly the first-born, as an event that does what?","o":["Undeniably changes, transforms and restructures their life","Brings continued joy for most parents from start to finish, without exception","Mainly affects the mother’s physical health","Has little lasting effect once the baby is weaned","Changes the mother’s life, but rarely the father’s"],"e":"“The birth of a child, in particular the first-born, is an event that <b>undeniably changes, transforms and restructures the parents' life</b>” (Silva & Carneiro 2014). Some parents feel continued joy, but most have mixed feelings.","w":["Some feel joy; most have mixed feelings.","Not the quote.","Not the quote.","It restructures the parents’ life."],"k":"First birth transforms parents' lives.","rn":[["","How does the birth of a child, especially the first-born, affect the parents' lives?","How does the birth of a child, especially the first-born, affect the parents' lives?","Changes, transforms and restructures them"]]},{"id":6053,"t":"Adaptation to Parenthood","s":"Name Winnicott’s concept that a mother does not need to be perfect to meet her infant’s needs.","e":"Winnicott (1953): the “good enough mother” – in contrast to the unrealistic “SuperMom” ideal.","k":"Good enough mother.","a":["good enough mother","the good enough mother","good enough mothering","good-enough mother"],"rn":[["","Whose concept is the \"good enough mother\"?","Whose concept is the \"good enough mother\"?","Winnicott (1953)"]]},{"id":6054,"t":"Adaptation to Parenthood","s":"According to Kim (2016), what is key to child developmental outcomes?","o":["The mother’s adaptation to parenthood","The father’s adaptation to parenthood","The baby’s birth weight","The number of antenatal classes attended","Exclusive breastfeeding for 6 months"],"e":"<b>The link between mother's adaptation to parenthood is key to child developmental outcomes</b> (Kim 2016).","w":["Kim (2016) focused on the mother.","Not stated.","Not stated.","Not stated."],"k":"Maternal adaptation shapes child development.","rn":[["","What is key to child developmental outcomes?","What is key to child developmental outcomes?","Mother's adaptation to parenthood; (Kim 2016)"]]},{"id":6055,"t":"Adaptation to Parenthood","s":"Which basic needs must mothers ensure are met across all cultures?","o":["To eat, sleep and be protected from dangers","To eat, sleep and be exclusively breastfed for 6 months","To eat, sleep and have their own room","To eat, sleep and receive early stimulation","To eat and be held in a sling"],"e":"Across all cultures, mothers have to ensure the newborn's basic needs are met: <b>eat, sleep, and protection</b> from environmental extremes and other dangers.","w":["Breastfeeding was not part of this list.","Not stated.","Not stated.","Not stated."],"k":"Newborn basics: food, sleep, protection.","rn":[["","What basic needs must mothers meet in every culture?","What basic needs must mothers meet in every culture?","Eating; Sleeping; Protection from extremes and dangers"]]},{"id":6056,"t":"Adaptation to Parenthood","s":"In Silva and Carneiro’s (2014) study, what single word did fathers use to describe their role around childbirth?","e":"Fathers felt they were “accessories”, with the mother in the lead role, unsure what was expected of them.","k":"Accessories.","a":["accessories","accessory"],"rn":[["","How did fathers feel in Silva & Carneiro (2014)?","How did fathers feel in Silva & Carneiro (2014)?","Like \"accessories\"; Unsure what was expected"]]},{"id":6057,"t":"Adaptation to Parenthood","s":"Why might some fathers be confused about their role today?","o":["They are no longer the sole breadwinner","They are now expected to be the main carer from birth","They are no longer allowed at births in most hospitals","They are expected to stay at home while mothers return to work","They cannot form attachments until the child is 2"],"e":"In recent decades the father's role has changed dramatically: no longer the <b>‘sole’ breadwinner and patriarch</b>, as women are more independent and <b>wage earners</b> in their own right, causing <b>confusion</b> for some, particularly around childbirth.","w":["Not stated.","Not stated.","Not stated.","Bonding with fathers is important."],"k":"Changing father role → confusion.","rn":[["","How has the father's role changed?","How has the father's role changed?","No longer sole breadwinner; Some confusion about role"]]},{"id":6058,"t":"Breastfeeding","s":"For how many months does the WHO advocate exclusive breastfeeding?","e":"WHO (2003) advocates exclusive breastfeeding for the first 6 months; under 20% in the UK achieve this.","k":"6 months.","a":["6","six","6 months","six months"],"rn":[["","UK breastfeeding figures","UK breastfeeding figures","~20% no breastfeeding; <20% exclusive for 6+ months"],["","What does the WHO advise on breastfeeding?","What does the WHO advise on breastfeeding?","Exclusive breastfeeding; First 6 months"]]},{"id":6059,"t":"Breastfeeding","s":"Which statement about breastfeeding rates in the UK is correct?","o":["About 20% don’t breastfeed; under 20% do so exclusively for 6 months","About 20% don’t breastfeed; over 50% do so exclusively for 6 months","About 50% don’t breastfeed; under 20% do so exclusively for 6 months","About 20% don’t breastfeed; under 5% intend to breastfeed","About 5% don’t breastfeed; most meet WHO guidance"],"e":"The majority <b>intend</b> to breastfeed but a <b>minority meet WHO guidance</b>: in the UK <b>~20% do not breastfeed</b>, <b>&lt;20% exclusively breastfeed for ≥6 months</b>, and <b>lower SES ≈ less breastfeeding</b>.","w":["A minority meet WHO guidance.","About 20% do not.","Most intend to.","A minority meet guidance."],"k":"UK: ~20% no BF; <20% exclusive ≥6 mo.","rn":[["","What % are exclusively breastfed for 6 months or more?","What % are exclusively breastfed for 6 months or more?","Less than 20%"]]},{"id":6060,"t":"Breastfeeding","s":"What can increase the initiation and/or continuation of breastfeeding?","o":["Interventions in the home or community","Formula samples given at hospital discharge","Delaying the first feed until milk comes in","Hospital-only lactation clinics after 6 weeks","Information leaflets given in the third trimester"],"e":"<b>Interventions in home or community can increase initiation and/or continuation of breastfeeding</b> (McAndrew 2012; McFadden 2017; Odar Stough 2019; Peregrino 2018).","w":["Not stated.","Not stated.","Home/community interventions.","Not stated."],"k":"Home/community interventions boost breastfeeding.","rn":[["","What increases breastfeeding initiation and continuation?","What increases breastfeeding initiation and continuation?","Interventions in home or community"]]},{"id":6061,"t":"Breastfeeding","s":"Which set lists the health benefits of breastfeeding for the baby given?","o":["Lower infectious mortality, less asthma, immunomodulatory milk","Lower infectious mortality, less diabetes, immunomodulatory milk","Higher birth weight, less asthma, immunomodulatory milk","Lower infectious mortality, less asthma, better birth spacing","Lower infectious mortality, fewer allergies, prebiotic formula"],"e":"Health benefits for the baby: <b>lower mortality due to infectious diseases</b>, <b>lower rates of asthma</b> and the <b>immunomodulatory qualities</b> of breast milk (Victora et al., 2016). Cognitive benefits are listed separately.","w":["Diabetes is a maternal benefit.","Not stated.","Birth spacing is maternal.","Not stated."],"k":"Baby health benefits: infection, asthma, immunomodulation.","rn":[["","What are the health benefits of breastfeeding for the baby?","What are the health benefits of breastfeeding for the baby?","Lower mortality from infectious disease; Lower rates of asthma; Immunomodulatory qualities of breast milk"]]},{"id":6062,"t":"Breastfeeding","s":"Which statement about the cognitive benefits of breastfeeding is correct (Horta et al. 2015, 2018)?","o":["Better “intelligence” scores into adulthood, stronger if disadvantaged","Better “intelligence” scores in childhood only, stronger if advantaged","Better “intelligence” scores into adulthood, stronger if advantaged","No difference in “intelligence” scores once SES is controlled","Better “intelligence” scores in childhood, fading by adolescence"],"e":"BF babies <b>perform better on “intelligence” tests in childhood and adulthood</b> (Horta 2015, 2018), and the <b>effect is stronger in disadvantaged children</b>.","w":["Also adulthood; stronger in disadvantaged.","Stronger in disadvantaged children.","There is an effect.","Into adulthood."],"k":"BF: better IQ test scores, stronger in disadvantaged children.","rn":[["","What are the cognitive benefits of breastfeeding?","What are the cognitive benefits of breastfeeding?","Better \"intelligence\" scores; In childhood and adulthood"]]},{"id":6063,"t":"Breastfeeding","s":"Breastfeeding mothers tend to be more educated, have higher SES and smoke less. What type of explanation is this for the breastfeeding–IQ link?","e":"The third explanation – other factors linked to both breastfeeding and cognitive development (education, SES, partner, smoking) – is a confounding explanation.","k":"Confounding.","a":["confounding","confounder","confounders","confounding factors","confounding explanation","confounding variable"]},{"id":6064,"t":"Breastfeeding","s":"Which statement describes the “nutrients in breast milk” explanation (Victora et al. 2016)?","o":["It transmits microbiome elements and immune responses, plus prebiotics","It contains extra calories that directly increase IQ scores","It transmits the mother’s antibodies but no microbiome elements","It contains prebiotics but transmits no immune responses","It works mainly by improving maternal SES"],"e":"Mother's breast milk <b>transmits elements of her own microbiome and immune responses</b> and provides <b>specific prebiotics to support growth of beneficial bacteria</b>.","w":["Not stated.","Microbiome too.","Immune responses too.","That is the confounding explanation."],"k":"Breast milk: microbiome, immune elements, prebiotics.","rn":[["","Which breast milk factors explain the cognitive benefit?","Which breast milk factors explain the cognitive benefit?","Microbiome; Immune responses; Prebiotics"]]},{"id":6065,"t":"Breastfeeding","s":"Which benefits of breastfeeding for the mother are listed (Victora et al. 2016)?","o":["Prevents breast cancer, improves birth spacing; may reduce diabetes risk","Prevents breast cancer, prevents PND; may reduce diabetes risk","Prevents ovarian cancer, improves birth spacing; may reduce asthma","Improves birth spacing, prevents baby blues; may reduce breast cancer","Prevents breast cancer, increases weight gain; may reduce diabetes"],"e":"Maternal benefits: <b>prevents breast cancer</b> (so quiz question 10 was true), <b>improves birth spacing</b> via <b>lactational amenorrhoea</b>, and <b>might reduce</b> risk of <b>diabetes and ovarian cancer</b>.","w":["PND prevention was not listed.","Breast cancer is prevented; ovarian might be reduced.","Breast cancer is prevented.","Not listed."],"k":"Maternal BF benefits: breast cancer, spacing, diabetes/ovarian cancer.","rn":[["","Which risks might breastfeeding reduce in mothers?","Which risks might breastfeeding reduce in mothers?","Diabetes; Ovarian cancer"],["","Which 3 maternal disease risks does breastfeeding lower?","Which 3 maternal disease risks does breastfeeding lower?","Breast cancer; Ovarian cancer; Type 2 diabetes"],["","Which cancer does breastfeeding prevent in mothers?","Which cancer does breastfeeding prevent in mothers?","Breast cancer"]]},{"id":6066,"t":"Breastfeeding","s":"Breastfeeding improves birth spacing by suppressing periods. Name this effect.","e":"Lactational amenorrhoea – absence of periods during breastfeeding – improves birth spacing.","k":"Lactational amenorrhoea.","a":["lactational amenorrhoea","lactational amenorrhea","lactational amenorrhoea method"],"rn":[["","How does breastfeeding help birth spacing?","How does breastfeeding help birth spacing?","Lactational amenorrhoea"]]},{"id":6067,"t":"Breastfeeding","s":"A study finds breastfed children score higher on intelligence tests. A critic says the mothers were also more educated and less likely to smoke. Which of the three explanations does the critic invoke?","o":["Other factors linked to both BF and cognition","Nutrients and microbiome elements in breast milk","Physical and psychological contact during feeding","Immunomodulatory qualities of breast milk","Lactational amenorrhoea and improved birth spacing"],"e":"Explanation 3: <b>other factors linked to both BF and cognitive development</b> – BF mothers have higher education, higher SES, are more likely to have a partner and less likely to smoke.","w":["Direct explanation.","Direct explanation.","A health benefit.","A maternal benefit, not an explanation."],"k":"Confounders explain part of the BF–IQ link.","rn":[["","Which linked factors may explain the cognitive benefit?","Which linked factors may explain the cognitive benefit?","Education; SES; Partner; Not smoking"],["","What are the 3 explanations for breastfeeding's cognitive benefit?","What are the 3 explanations for breastfeeding's cognitive benefit?","Nutrients in breast milk; Physical and psychological contact; Other linked factors"]]},{"id":6068,"t":"Breastfeeding","s":"Which explanation of the breastfeeding–cognition link connects to attachment theory?","o":["Physical and psychological contact during feeding","Nutrients and microbiome elements transmitted in breast milk","Higher maternal education and SES among breastfeeding mothers","Immunomodulatory qualities of breast milk","Prebiotics that support beneficial bacteria"],"e":"Explanation 2: <b>physical and psychological contact during BF contributes to cognitive development – e.g. via secure attachment</b>.","w":["Not linked to attachment.","Confounding.","Not attachment.","Not attachment."],"k":"Contact during BF → secure attachment → cognition.","rn":[["","What are the 3 explanations for breastfeeding's cognitive benefit?","What are the 3 explanations for breastfeeding's cognitive benefit?","Nutrients in breast milk; Physical and psychological contact; Other linked factors"]]},{"id":6069,"t":"Mental Health in Pregnancy","s":"A woman with depression during pregnancy asks if it matters for her baby if it goes untreated. What is the best answer?","o":["Yes: LBW, preterm birth, later ADHD/conduct problems","Yes: but only for postnatal outcomes, not the baby itself","No: pregnancy protects the baby from the effects of maternal mental illness","No: only depression after birth affects the baby","Yes: but mainly higher birth weight and longer gestation"],"e":"Untreated depression is associated with <b>LBW</b>, <b>preterm delivery</b> and adverse child outcomes (<b>emotional problems, ADHD, conduct disorder</b>). Poor mental health in pregnancy is also a <b>strong predictor of postnatal mental illness</b>.","w":["Antenatal outcomes too.","Not protective.","Antenatal depression matters.","LBW and preterm."],"k":"Antenatal depression has foetal and child consequences.","rn":[["","Untreated depression in pregnancy: adverse outcomes for the child?","Untreated depression in pregnancy: adverse outcomes for the child?","Emotional problems; ADHD; Conduct disorder"]]},{"id":6070,"t":"Baby Blues and Postnatal Depression","s":"A woman's low mood began during pregnancy and continued 6 weeks after birth. Which statement is most accurate?","o":["This fits PND: a third of PND began in pregnancy","This fits baby blues, which can last 6 weeks","This cannot be PND, as PND starts after birth","This fits PTSD after a traumatic birth","This fits tokophobia continuing after birth"],"e":"PND onset is usually within 1–2 months, and <b>1/3 of women with PND have symptoms which started in pregnancy and continue after birth</b> (Wisner 2013). Baby blues stops by day 10.","w":["Baby blues stop by day 10.","A third started in pregnancy.","Not described.","Not described."],"k":"PND can begin in pregnancy.","rn":[["","What fraction of women with PND had symptoms starting in pregnancy?","What fraction of women with PND had symptoms starting in pregnancy?","1/3 (Wisner 2013)"]]},{"id":6071,"t":"Baby Blues and Postnatal Depression","s":"Which pairing of condition and frequency is correct?","o":["Baby blues – about half of women; PND – 10–15%","Baby blues – 10–15%; PND – about half of women","Baby blues – about half of women; PND – about a quarter","Baby blues – about a quarter; PND – 13%","Baby blues – about half of women; PND – 45%"],"e":"<b>Baby blues</b>: about <b>½</b> of women (Henshaw 2003). <b>PND</b>: <b>10–15%</b> of mothers (Woody 2017). Antenatal anxiety: 13%. PTSD symptoms: ¼ after vaginal delivery.","w":["Reversed.","PND 10–15%.","Baby blues about half.","45% is unplanned pregnancy."],"k":"Baby blues ½; PND 10–15%.","rn":[["","How many women get baby blues?","How many women get baby blues?","About 1/2 (Henshaw 2003)"]]},{"id":6072,"t":"Support in Pregnancy and Labour","s":"A hospital wants to reduce traumatic birth experiences and PND. Which intervention is best supported?","o":["Providing support in pregnancy and birth","Providing more routine intervention in labour","Providing information leaflets in the third trimester","Providing formula to reduce feeding stress","Providing separate rooms for mother and baby"],"e":"Support in pregnancy and birth (Hodnett et al., 2013) gives <b>greater perceived control</b>, <b>less stress</b>, <b>greater satisfaction</b>, <b>lower likelihood of traumatic birth</b> and <b>lower likelihood of PND</b>.","w":["Not supported.","Not the evidence given.","Not supported.","Not supported."],"k":"Support reduces traumatic birth and PND.","rn":[["","Which 3 benefits of support help during pregnancy and birth?","Which 3 benefits of support help during pregnancy and birth?","Greater perceived control; Less stress; Greater birth satisfaction"],["","Who showed support in pregnancy and birth is important?","Who showed support in pregnancy and birth is important?","Hodnett et al. (2013)"]]},{"id":6073,"t":"Parent-Infant Bonding","s":"An adopted child had little contact with carers in early infancy. Which view is supported?","o":["Children have some resilience; earlier intervention gives better outcomes","Children have some resilience; later intervention gives better outcomes","The child cannot form attachments after the first 6 weeks","The child can attach only to biological parents","Early contact has no effect on later development"],"e":"<b>All is not lost</b> if there is less contact in early life (e.g. adopted children); <b>children have some resilience</b> (Rutter 2008), and <b>outcomes are better with earlier intervention</b>.","w":["Earlier is better.","All is not lost.","Not stated.","Contact matters."],"k":"Resilience; early intervention helps.","rn":[["","What does Rutter (2008) say about children?","What does Rutter (2008) say about children?","Some resilience; Better with earlier intervention"]]},{"id":6074,"t":"Mental Health in Pregnancy","s":"A 24-year-old at 30 weeks has untreated depression, smokes to cope and has little partner support. Which combination of risks applies?","o":["Risk of LBW and preterm birth now, and of PND afterwards","Risk of high birth weight now, and of baby blues only afterwards","No foetal risk, as pregnancy protects against mental illness","Risk of PND only, as antenatal depression does not affect the baby","Risk of tokophobia only, as she is in the third trimester"],"e":"Untreated depression in pregnancy is associated with low birth weight and preterm delivery, and later emotional problems, ADHD and conduct disorder. Poor mental health in pregnancy strongly predicts postnatal illness; depression in pregnancy and poor partner support are risk factors for PND. Women with mental illness are more likely to misuse nicotine and alcohol.","w":["Untreated depression is linked to low, not high, birth weight; and her risk extends to PND.","Pregnancy is not protective against mental illness.","Untreated antenatal depression is linked to LBW, preterm delivery and later child problems.","Tokophobia is an extreme fear of childbirth, not implied here."],"k":"Antenatal depression: foetal risks + PND risk.","rn":[["","Which 2 mental health histories raise PND risk?","Which 2 mental health histories raise PND risk?","Previous mental health problems; Depression/anxiety in pregnancy"],["","Untreated depression in pregnancy: risks to the baby?","Untreated depression in pregnancy: risks to the baby?","Low birth weight; Pre-term delivery"],["","PND increases the risk of what?","PND increases the risk of what?","Later depression"]]},{"id":6075,"t":"Mental Health in Pregnancy","s":"A mother is depressed and seems less sensitive to her baby's emotional cues. Which downstream effect on the infant is most likely?","o":["Poorer learning and cognition, more attachment problems","Poorer infant learning, but stronger secure attachment","Faster language development to compensate","Poorer physical growth, but normal cognitive development","Earlier separation anxiety, but better cognitive development overall"],"e":"Postnatal depression → <b>poor engagement with infants</b> → <b>poor infant learning and cognitive development</b>; depressed mothers are less sensitive to emotions and attachment problems are more likely (Murray et al. 2014).","w":["Attachment problems are more likely.","Not stated.","Cognitive development is poorer.","Cognition is poorer."],"k":"Maternal depression → poorer infant development.","rn":[["","How does postnatal depression affect the infant?","How does postnatal depression affect the infant?","Poor engagement with infant; Poorer infant learning; Poorer cognitive development"]]},{"id":6076,"t":"Adaptation to Parenthood","s":"Which statement about the father's role around childbirth is correct?","o":["It has changed; some fathers are unsure what is expected","The role is unchanged; fathers remain the sole breadwinner","It has changed, but fathers still have little part in bonding with the baby","The role has changed, and fathers are now the lead carer","The role has changed, and most fathers feel fully confident"],"e":"The father's role has <b>changed dramatically</b>; no longer the sole breadwinner; some fathers feel like <b>“accessories”</b> and are unsure what is expected. Bonding and attachment with the father is important, and PND is not only in biological mothers.","w":["Changed.","Bonding with fathers is important.","Mothers play the lead role.","Many are unsure."],"k":"Fathers' changing role and uncertainty.","rn":[["","How did fathers feel in Silva & Carneiro (2014)?","How did fathers feel in Silva & Carneiro (2014)?","Like \"accessories\"; Unsure what was expected"],["","How has the father's role changed?","How has the father's role changed?","No longer sole breadwinner; Some confusion about role"]]},{"id":6077,"t":"Influences on Pregnancy","s":"Which risk factor appears both for unplanned pregnancy and as a harmful behavioural influence in pregnancy?","o":["Substance misuse","Higher education","Older age","Breastfeeding","Continuous labour support"],"e":"<b>Smoking and substance misuse</b> is a risk factor for unplanned pregnancy (PHE 2018), and <b>alcohol and other drugs</b> are behavioural influences associated with foetal/infant mortality, difficult labour and low birth weight.","w":["Lower education is a risk.","Younger age is a risk.","Protective.","Protective."],"k":"Substance misuse: unplanned pregnancy + poor outcomes.","rn":[["","What are the risk factors for unplanned pregnancy?","What are the risk factors for unplanned pregnancy?","Lower educational attainment; Younger age; Smoking and substance misuse"]]},{"id":6078,"t":"Influences on Pregnancy","s":"Which statement best explains why lower SES might produce poorer pregnancy outcomes?","o":["Lower SES: more maternal and infant morbidity/mortality, and less breastfeeding","Lower SES: more maternal and infant morbidity/mortality, but more breastfeeding","Lower SES: less infant morbidity, but more low birth weight","Higher SES: more low birth weight, but more breastfeeding","Lower SES: affects breastfeeding, but not morbidity or mortality"],"e":"Socioeconomic disadvantage increases risk of <b>maternal morbidity/mortality</b>, <b>LBW</b> and <b>infant morbidity/mortality</b> (de Graaf 2013), and <b>lower SES ≈ less breastfeeding</b>; breastfeeding mothers tend to have higher SES.","w":["Lower SES ≈ less breastfeeding.","More infant morbidity.","Not correct.","It affects morbidity/mortality too."],"k":"SES links to outcomes and breastfeeding.","rn":[["","How does SES affect breastfeeding?","How does SES affect breastfeeding?","Lower SES, less breastfeeding"],["","Which maternal risks does socioeconomic disadvantage raise?","Which maternal risks does socioeconomic disadvantage raise?","Maternal morbidity; Maternal mortality"],["","Which infant risks does socioeconomic disadvantage raise?","Which infant risks does socioeconomic disadvantage raise?","Low birth weight; Infant morbidity and mortality"]]},{"id":6079,"t":"Influences on Pregnancy","s":"How can maternal stress reach the foetus through the placental blood supply?","o":["The mother’s stress response (hormones, BP) can reach the foetus via it","Stress increases placental blood supply, which benefits the foetus","The placental blood supply is protected from maternal stress","Stress affects the foetus only through the mother’s behaviour","The placental blood supply carries the father’s stress hormones"],"e":"The <b>direct</b> stress pathway: a physiological response in the mother (<b>hormonal changes, BP</b>) can affect the foetus via the <b>placental blood supply</b>.","w":["Not stated.","It can be affected.","There is also a direct route.","Not stated."],"k":"Direct stress pathway via placental blood supply.","rn":[["","What does the direct physiological stress route involve?","What does the direct physiological stress route involve?","Hormones; BP (blood pressure); Placental blood supply"]]},{"id":6080,"t":"Parent-Infant Bonding","s":"A mother's newborn quickly calms when held close to her rather than a stranger. Which bonding mechanisms best explain this?","o":["Her smell linked with comfort, and a preference for her voice","Separation anxiety from strangers, present from birth","Clear-cut attachment to the mother, present from birth","Recognition of the mother’s face, present from 3 months","Her smell linked with danger in strangers"],"e":"Babies quickly learn to associate their <b>mother's smell</b> (pheromones) with comfort, pleasure and food, and have a <b>predisposition and preference for the mother's voice</b>.","w":["Separation anxiety is at 7 months.","Clear-cut is 7 months–2 years.","Not the mechanism given.","Smell is linked with comfort."],"k":"Smell and voice drive early bonding.","rn":[["","Smell in bonding: what do babies learn?","Smell in bonding: what do babies learn?","Link mother's smell (pheromones); With comfort, pleasure, food"]]},{"id":6081,"t":"Attachment","s":"Which statement about attachment in the pre-attachment stage (0–6 weeks) is correct?","o":["Attachment behaviour is non-specific","There is strong separation anxiety","Attachment behaviours are directed only to parents","Separation anxiety diminishes","The baby has a reciprocal relationship"],"e":"<b>Pre-attachment (0–6 wk)</b>: <b>non-specific attachment behaviour</b>.","w":["Clear-cut stage.","Clear-cut stage.","Reciprocal stage.","From 2 years."],"k":"Pre-attachment: non-specific behaviour.","rn":[["","Pre-attachment stage: age and feature?","Pre-attachment stage: age and feature?","0-6 weeks; Non-specific attachment behaviour"]]},{"id":6082,"t":"Attachment","s":"Name the attachment stage (7 months–2 years) in which separation anxiety appears.","e":"Clear-cut attachment: behaviours directed to specific people, separation anxiety, better ability to maintain contact with preferred people.","k":"Clear-cut attachment.","a":["clear-cut attachment","clear cut attachment","clear-cut","clearcut attachment"],"rn":[["","When does separation anxiety appear?","When does separation anxiety appear?","Clear-cut attachment stage; 7 months - 2 years"],["","What are the features of clear-cut attachment?","What are the features of clear-cut attachment?","Behaviour at specific people; Esp. parents; Separation anxiety"],["","What ages are clear-cut attachment and reciprocal relationship?","What ages are clear-cut attachment and reciprocal relationship?","Clear-cut: 7 months-2 years; Reciprocal: 2 years +"]]},{"id":6083,"t":"Anxiety in Pregnancy","s":"Which statement about anxiety and depression in the perinatal period is correct?","o":["They can occur alone or together; many women experience both","They rarely occur together in the perinatal period","Anxiety occurs only after birth, depression only during pregnancy","Anxiety affects about 45% of pregnant women","Depression always precedes anxiety in the perinatal period"],"e":"<b>13%</b> experience anxiety in pregnancy; <b>many women experience both</b> anxiety and depression (NICE 2014); anxiety disorders <b>can occur on their own or coexist with depression</b>.","w":["They often coexist.","Both can occur in pregnancy.","13%.","Not stated."],"k":"Perinatal anxiety and depression often coexist.","rn":[["","Can anxiety and depression coexist in pregnancy?","Can anxiety and depression coexist in pregnancy?","Yes, many women have both"],["","What % of women experience anxiety in pregnancy?","What % of women experience anxiety in pregnancy?","13% (NICE 2014)"]]},{"id":6084,"t":"Adaptation to Parenthood","s":"What does the idealised “SuperMom” (juggling kids, chores and career with an “invisible third arm”) represent?","o":["An unrealistic ideal, unlike the “good enough mother”","A realistic standard that most mothers should aim to reach","A description of the father’s changing role","A NICE description of maternal coping","An ideal that supports “it takes a village”"],"e":"The “SuperMom” passage describes an impossible ideal (extra hours, invisible third arm). It contrasts with Winnicott's <b>“good enough mother”</b> and <b>“it takes a village to raise a child”</b>.","w":["Unrealistic.","About mothers.","Not NICE.","It contrasts with it."],"k":"SuperMom vs good enough mother.","rn":[["","Whose concept is the \"good enough mother\"?","Whose concept is the \"good enough mother\"?","Winnicott (1953)"]]},{"id":6085,"t":"Adaptation to Parenthood","s":"What does “it takes a village to raise a child” emphasise?","o":["Mothers need support from others to meet the infant’s needs","Mothers should meet all of the infant’s needs themselves","Children are best raised by professionals rather than parents","Fathers should take the lead role in raising the child","Mothers need to be perfect to meet the infant’s needs"],"e":"Alongside the “good enough mother”, the saying emphasises that raising a child needs <b>support from others</b> – consistent with the lecture's emphasis on social support.","w":["The opposite.","Not stated.","Not stated.","Good enough is enough."],"k":"Raising children needs wider support.","rn":[["","What basic needs must mothers meet in every culture?","What basic needs must mothers meet in every culture?","Eating; Sleeping; Protection from extremes and dangers"]]},{"id":6086,"t":"Baby Blues and Postnatal Depression","s":"A refugee mother who recently arrived in the UK has no family support and a history of abuse. Which statement is most accurate?","o":["She has several recognised risk factors for postnatal depression","She has risk factors for baby blues, but not for PND","She has few risk factors, as social factors matter less than biology","She has risk factors for PND, but screening is not advised","She has risk factors only for antenatal anxiety"],"e":"Risk factors for PND include arriving in a developed country as a <b>refugee or to seek asylum</b>, <b>poor support</b>, and <b>previous abuse/domestic violence</b> (Howard 2014).","w":["They are PND risk factors.","Social factors are risk factors.","Screening is appropriate.","PND risk factors."],"k":"Refugee status, isolation and abuse raise PND risk.","rn":[["","Which 2 relationship factors raise PND risk?","Which 2 relationship factors raise PND risk?","Poor support or relationship difficulties; Domestic violence or previous abuse"],["","Which 2 other risk factors raise PND risk?","Which 2 other risk factors raise PND risk?","Recent stressful event; Refugee or asylum seeker"],["","PND","PND","Postnatal Depression"]]},{"id":6087,"t":"Attachment","s":"A 10-month-old cries when his mother leaves the room and reaches for her when she returns, but is wary of a new nanny. Which attachment stage and features fit?","o":["Clear-cut attachment: behaviours to specific people, with separation anxiety","Attachment in the making: developing preference, no separation anxiety yet","Pre-attachment: non-specific behaviours, no separation anxiety","Reciprocal relationship: separation anxiety diminishing","Clear-cut attachment: non-specific behaviours to any adult"],"e":"Clear-cut attachment (7 months–2 years): attachment behaviours directed to specific people, separation anxiety, and better ability to maintain contact with preferred people. Separation anxiety diminishes in the reciprocal relationship stage (2 years+).","w":["Attachment in the making (6 weeks–7 months) has a developing preference but not yet separation anxiety.","Pre-attachment is 0–6 weeks.","Reciprocal relationship starts from 2 years.","In clear-cut attachment behaviours are directed to specific people."],"k":"7 mo–2 yr: clear-cut attachment, separation anxiety.","rn":[["","What are the features of clear-cut attachment?","What are the features of clear-cut attachment?","Behaviour at specific people; Esp. parents; Separation anxiety"]]},{"id":6088,"t":"Attachment","s":"Name the first stage of attachment (0–6 weeks), with non-specific attachment behaviour.","e":"Stages: pre-attachment (0–6 weeks), attachment in the making (6 weeks–7 months), clear-cut attachment (7 months–2 years), reciprocal relationship (2 years+).","k":"Pre-attachment.","a":["pre-attachment","pre attachment","preattachment"],"rn":[["","What ages are pre-attachment and attachment in the making?","What ages are pre-attachment and attachment in the making?","Pre-attachment: 0-6 weeks; Making: 6 weeks-7 months"],["","What are the first 2 stages of attachment?","What are the first 2 stages of attachment?","Pre-attachment; Attachment in the making"],["","Pre-attachment stage: age and feature?","Pre-attachment stage: age and feature?","0-6 weeks; Non-specific attachment behaviour"]]},{"id":6089,"t":"Emotions in Each Trimester","s":"Which statement about the mother's sense of loss of independence is correct?","o":["It can drive negative feelings in the first trimester","It drives negative feelings mainly in the third trimester","It is a recognised risk factor for postnatal PTSD","It typically appears only after the baby is born","It is one of the anxiety disorders, like tokophobia"],"e":"First-trimester emotions depend on factors including a <b>perception of lifestyle restriction</b> and a <b>sense of loss of independence</b>.","w":["Third trimester is discomfort and insomnia.","Not stated.","It is a first-trimester factor.","Not an anxiety disorder."],"k":"Loss of independence → first-trimester negative feelings.","rn":[["","Second trimester: what happens to negative feelings?","Second trimester: what happens to negative feelings?","They can lessen"],["","Third trimester: what happens to emotions?","Third trimester: what happens to emotions?","Negative feelings can come back"],["","Why do negative feelings lessen in the second trimester?","Why do negative feelings lessen in the second trimester?","Less nausea and vomiting; More adaptation; Some idea about pregnancy care"]]},{"id":6090,"t":"Parent-Infant Bonding","s":"Why may babies still bond well despite a stay in an incubator?","o":["Kangaroo care has advantages for preterm infants","Incubators let the baby learn the mother’s smell","Incubators allow constant eye contact with the mother","Separation anxiety does not develop in incubator babies","Bonding is unaffected by the amount of contact"],"e":"For children in incubators, <b>kangaroo care</b> has advantages (Kambarami, 1998), and children have some resilience.","w":["Not stated.","Not stated.","Not stated.","Contact matters."],"k":"Kangaroo care helps preterm bonding.","rn":[["","What helps incubator babies bond?","What helps incubator babies bond?","Kangaroo care (Kambarami 1998)"]]},{"id":6091,"t":"Mental Health in Pregnancy","s":"Which statement about PTSD after childbirth is correct?","o":["About a quarter after vaginal deliveries, as birth is emotionally intense","About a quarter after caesarean sections only","About half after vaginal deliveries, as birth is emotionally intense","About a quarter after vaginal deliveries, the same as baby blues","About 13% after vaginal deliveries, as birth is emotionally intense"],"e":"The experience of childbirth is <b>emotionally intense</b>; <b>PTSD symptoms in ¼ of women after vaginal deliveries</b> (Olde et al., 2006).","w":["After vaginal deliveries.","About a quarter.","PTSD is not baby blues.","13% is antenatal anxiety."],"k":"¼ PTSD symptoms after vaginal birth.","rn":[["","What % of women have PTSD symptoms after vaginal delivery?","What % of women have PTSD symptoms after vaginal delivery?","About 1/4; (Olde et al. 2006)"]]},{"id":6092,"t":"Support in Pregnancy and Labour","s":"Which pair of outcomes is linked specifically to <b>continuous support in labour</b>?","o":["Shorter labour and better Apgar scores","Lower likelihood of PND and greater satisfaction","Greater perceived control and less stress","Lower likelihood of traumatic birth","Higher breastfeeding rates"],"e":"<b>Continuous support in labour</b>: <b>shorter labour</b>, <b>less medical/surgical intervention</b>, <b>better Apgar scores</b>. The others belong to general support in pregnancy and birth.","w":["General support.","General support.","General support.","Not stated."],"k":"Continuous labour support: shorter labour, better Apgar.","rn":[["","What are the benefits of continuous support in labour?","What are the benefits of continuous support in labour?","Shorter labour; Less medical / surgical intervention; Better Apgar scores"]]},{"id":6093,"t":"Influences on Pregnancy","s":"Which statement about information and knowledge in pregnancy is supported?","o":["Information provision can change health behaviour","Information provision mainly increases anxiety","Information provision replaces the need for support","Information is useful only in the third trimester","Information has little effect on health behaviour"],"e":"<b>Provision of information can change health behaviour</b>, the basis for antenatal classes; the second trimester also brings “some idea about the pregnancy care” from professionals.","w":["Not stated.","Not stated.","Not stated.","It can change behaviour."],"k":"Information → behaviour change.","rn":[["","What is the basis for antenatal classes?","What is the basis for antenatal classes?","Information can change; Health behaviour"]]},{"id":6094,"t":"Attachment","s":"Which statement about the impact of neglect is correct?","o":["Neglect can impair the emotional brain and language areas","Neglect mainly impairs physical growth, not the brain","Neglect strengthens resilience in later childhood","Neglect affects the occipital lobes (vision) most","Neglect has effects only after 2 years of age"],"e":"Quality of attachment affects neurological development of the <b>limbic system</b> (emotional brain) and <b>temporal lobes</b> (language); the slides then highlight <b>the impact of neglect</b>.","w":["Limbic and temporal lobes.","Not stated.","Limbic and temporal.","Not stated."],"k":"Neglect affects attachment-dependent brain development.","rn":[["","Which brain areas does attachment quality affect?","Which brain areas does attachment quality affect?","Limbic system (emotional brain); Temporal lobes (language)"]]},{"id":6095,"t":"Breastfeeding","s":"A health visitor wants to boost exclusive breastfeeding in a low-SES area. Which approach is supported?","o":["Home or community interventions","Hospital-based classes during pregnancy only","Advising early formula alongside breastfeeding","Waiting until 6 months to discuss feeding","Leaflets about WHO guidance at discharge"],"e":"Lower SES ≈ less breastfeeding, and <b>interventions in home or community can increase initiation and/or continuation</b> of breastfeeding.","w":["Home or community.","Not supported.","Not supported.","Not supported."],"k":"Community interventions boost BF.","rn":[["","What increases breastfeeding initiation and continuation?","What increases breastfeeding initiation and continuation?","Interventions in home or community"]]},{"id":6096,"t":"Perspectives on Pregnancy and Birth","s":"Which of these is a <b>psychological</b> perspective on birth rather than a biomedical one?","o":["An experience, with a focus on satisfaction and fulfilment","A medical event, with a focus on potential problems","A normal physiological event, with minimal intervention","A socially determined event, e.g. increased intervention","A medical event, with a focus on illness and pathology"],"e":"Psychological perspective: birth is an <b>experience</b>; focus = <b>satisfaction and fulfilment</b>. Biomedical: medical event, illness, pathology, potential problems.","w":["Biomedical.","Physiological.","Social.","Biomedical."],"k":"Psychological view: experience and fulfilment.","rn":[["","Psychological perspective on birth?","Psychological perspective on birth?","An experience; Focus on satisfaction and fulfilment"]]},{"id":6097,"t":"Emotions in Each Trimester","s":"Which statement about the second trimester is <b>false</b>?","o":["Mood fluctuations stop completely","Negative feelings could sometimes lessen","Nausea and vomiting are reduced","There is more adaptation to changes","Anxiety and depression occur less commonly than in the other trimesters"],"e":"<b>Mood fluctuations continue</b> in the second trimester, but negative feelings may lessen.","w":["True.","True.","True.","True (Lee 2007)."],"k":"Second trimester: fluctuations continue."},{"id":6098,"t":"Emotions in Each Trimester","s":"Which pair of factors explains why the third trimester can bring back negative emotions?","o":["Pelvic girdle pain/backache and exhaustion","Nausea/vomiting and loss of independence","Unplanned pregnancy and financial worries","Lifestyle restriction and family support","Reduced nausea and more information"],"e":"Third trimester: <b>increasing discomfort</b> (pelvic girdle pain/backache), <b>insomnia</b> and <b>tiredness/exhaustion</b>.","w":["First trimester.","First trimester.","First trimester.","Second trimester (positive)."],"k":"Third trimester discomfort and fatigue.","rn":[["","What discomfort occurs in the third trimester?","What discomfort occurs in the third trimester?","Pelvic girdle pain; Backache"]]},{"id":6099,"t":"Influences on Pregnancy","s":"Which explanation best accounts for why antenatal mental illness can lead to poor child outcomes through both biology and behaviour?","o":["Direct effects via hormones/BP; indirect via behaviour","Direct effects via the mother’s behaviour, and indirect effects via hormones and BP","Direct effects via genetics, and indirect effects via breastfeeding","Direct effects via hormones only, with no behavioural route","Indirect effects via behaviour only, with no physiological route"],"e":"The stress model shows a <b>direct physiological route</b> (hormonal changes, BP, placental blood supply) and an <b>indirect psychological route</b> (mother's behaviour). Women with mental illness are also <b>more likely to misuse alcohol, nicotine</b>, etc.","w":["Reversed.","Not the model.","There is a behavioural route.","There is a physiological route."],"k":"Direct + indirect routes from maternal state to foetus.","rn":[["","How does the indirect psychological stress route work?","How does the indirect psychological stress route work?","Via the mother's behaviour"],["","What are the 2 routes by which stress affects the foetus?","What are the 2 routes by which stress affects the foetus?","Direct physiological; Indirect psychological"],["","What does the direct physiological stress route involve?","What does the direct physiological stress route involve?","Hormones; BP (blood pressure); Placental blood supply"]]},{"id":6100,"t":"Anxiety in Pregnancy","s":"Which percentages apply to PND and to depression/anxiety in the first postnatal year?","o":["PND 10–15%; depression/anxiety in first year 15–20%","PND 15–20%; depression/anxiety in first year 10–15%","PND 10–15%; depression/anxiety in first year 13%","PND 13%; depression/anxiety in first year 15–20%","PND about half; depression/anxiety in first year 25%"],"e":"PND: <b>10–15%</b> (Woody 2017). Depression and anxiety in the first year after birth: <b>15–20%</b>.","w":["Reversed.","13% is antenatal anxiety.","PND 10–15%.","Not correct."],"k":"PND 10–15%; first-year dep/anx 15–20%.","rn":[["","What % of women have depression and anxiety in the first year after birth?","What % of women have depression and anxiety in the first year after birth?","15-20%"]]},{"id":6101,"t":"Baby Blues and Postnatal Depression","s":"Which pairing of researcher and finding is correct?","o":["Henshaw (2003) – baby blues in about half, from day 3–4","Olde (2006) – baby blues in about half, from day 3–4","Henshaw (2003) – PTSD symptoms in a quarter of women","Woody (2017) – 45% of pregnancies unplanned","Hodnett (2013) – breastfeeding and IQ scores"],"e":"Henshaw (2003): baby blues. Lee (2007): second-trimester mental health. Olde (2006): PTSD in ¼. Woody (2017): PND 10–15%. PHE (2018): 45% unplanned. Hodnett (2013): support. Horta: BF and IQ.","w":["Olde studied PTSD.","Olde.","PHE (2018).","Hodnett studied support."],"k":"Match researchers to findings.","rn":[["","How many women get baby blues?","How many women get baby blues?","About 1/2 (Henshaw 2003)"]]},{"id":6102,"t":"Breastfeeding","s":"Which statement about breastfeeding intention in the UK is supported?","o":["Most intend to breastfeed, but a minority meet WHO guidance","Most intend to breastfeed, and most meet WHO guidance","A minority intend to breastfeed, and few meet WHO guidance","Intention is highest in lower-SES groups","Intention and practice usually match"],"e":"The <b>majority intend to breastfeed</b>, but a <b>minority meet WHO guidance</b>.","w":["A minority meet it.","Most intend to.","Lower SES ≈ less breastfeeding.","They often differ."],"k":"Intention–practice gap in breastfeeding.","rn":[["","Do most mothers meet WHO (World Health Organization) guidance?","Do most mothers meet WHO (World Health Organization) guidance?","No, only a minority"],["","Do most mothers intend to breastfeed?","Do most mothers intend to breastfeed?","Yes, most intend to"]]},{"id":6103,"t":"Breastfeeding","s":"Why is the effect of breastfeeding on intelligence scores stronger in disadvantaged children an important finding?","o":["BF may help reduce inequalities in cognitive outcomes","Breastfeeding matters only for disadvantaged children and not for others","SES fully explains the link with intelligence scores","Formula is better for advantaged children","The finding shows breastfeeding has no real effect"],"e":"The effect is <b>stronger in disadvantaged children</b>; combined with lower SES ≈ less breastfeeding, this highlights the value of supporting breastfeeding in disadvantaged groups.","w":["Not stated.","Not stated.","Not stated.","There is an effect."],"k":"BF benefit strongest where disadvantage is greatest."},{"id":6104,"t":"Mental Health in Pregnancy","s":"What does ADHD stand for, and how does it relate to perinatal mental health?","o":["Attention deficit hyperactivity disorder; a child outcome","Attention deficit hyperactivity disorder; a cause of postnatal depression","Attention deficit hyperactivity disorder; a perinatal anxiety disorder","Attachment deficit and hyperactivity disorder; a child outcome of neglect","Antenatal depression and hormone disorder; a cause of baby blues"],"e":"<b>ADHD = attention deficit hyperactivity disorder</b>, listed as an adverse child outcome (with emotional problems and conduct disorder) linked to untreated depression in pregnancy.","w":["It is a child outcome.","Not an anxiety disorder.","Wrong expansion.","Wrong expansion."],"k":"ADHD as an outcome of untreated antenatal depression.","rn":[["","What does ADHD stand for?","What does ADHD stand for?","Attention Deficit Hyperactivity Disorder"]]},{"id":6105,"t":"Parent-Infant Bonding","s":"Which statement about the newborn's preference for the mother's voice is correct?","o":["Newborns are predisposed to prefer the mother’s voice","Newborns prefer the father’s voice at first","Newborns respond to voices only after 6 weeks","Voice preference develops in clear-cut attachment","Newborns prefer unfamiliar voices"],"e":"Sound: <b>predisposition and preference for mother's voice</b>.","w":["Mother’s voice.","Present from birth.","Present from birth.","Not stated."],"k":"Newborns prefer mother's voice.","rn":[["","Sound in bonding: what do babies prefer?","Sound in bonding: what do babies prefer?","Their mother's voice"]]},{"id":6106,"t":"Baby Blues and Postnatal Depression","s":"Which statement about baby blues is <b>false</b>?","o":["It requires antidepressant treatment","It affects about half of women","It begins usually on day 3 or 4","Women feel mildly depressed when they expect to feel happy","It usually stops by day 10"],"e":"Baby blues: <b>no treatment necessary</b>. It affects ~½ of women, starts day 3–4, involves feeling mildly depressed when expecting happiness, and stops by day 10.","w":["True.","True.","True.","True."],"k":"Baby blues needs no treatment."},{"id":6107,"t":"Perspectives on Pregnancy and Birth","s":"Which statement about the social perspective on birth is correct?","o":["Childbirth type is socially determined (e.g. intervention)","Birth is a normal physiological event, with minimal intervention where possible","Birth is an experience, focused on satisfaction and fulfilment","Birth is a medical event, focused on potential problems","Birth and motherhood are fulfilling"],"e":"<b>Social</b> perspective: the <b>type of childbirth is socially determined</b>, e.g. increased intervention.","w":["Physiological.","Psychological.","Biomedical.","Cultural."],"k":"Social perspective: childbirth type socially determined.","rn":[["","What is the social perspective on birth?","What is the social perspective on birth?","Type of childbirth is; Socially determined; e.g. more intervention"]]},{"id":6108,"t":"Adaptation to Parenthood","s":"A first-time mother asks whether her mixed feelings about becoming a parent are abnormal. What is the best answer?","o":["Most have mixed feelings; fear of parenthood is common","Most feel continued joy; mixed feelings suggest PND","Most have mixed feelings, which suggest poor attachment to the baby","Mixed feelings occur mainly in fathers","Mixed feelings mean she is not a “good enough mother”"],"e":"Some parents may feel <b>continued joy from start to finish</b>, but <b>most will have mixed feelings</b>; the most common anxiety is <b>fear of parenthood</b>.","w":["Most have mixed feelings.","Not stated.","Not stated.","Not stated."],"k":"Mixed feelings are common.","rn":[["","What is the most common parental anxiety?","What is the most common parental anxiety?","Fear of parenthood"],["","How do most parents feel about having a baby?","How do most parents feel about having a baby?","Mixed feelings"]]},{"id":6109,"t":"Support in Pregnancy and Labour","s":"Which of the following is <b>not</b> a recognised source of support for pregnant women and new mothers?","o":["Social media influencers","Midwife","Health visitor","GP","Support groups (e.g. NCT)"],"e":"Support sources shown: family, friends, GP, midwife, health visitor, support groups (e.g. NCT) and counselling.","w":["Listed.","Listed.","Listed.","Listed."],"k":"Know the support sources."},{"id":6110,"t":"Breastfeeding","s":"Which statement links attachment and breastfeeding?","o":["Contact during feeding may promote secure attachment and cognition","Breast milk nutrients promote secure attachment directly","Secure attachment determines how much milk is produced","Breastfeeding has no psychological component","Formula feeding promotes attachment more than breastfeeding"],"e":"One explanation of the BF–cognition link: <b>physical and psychological contact during BF contributes to cognitive development, e.g. via secure attachment</b>.","w":["Contact is the attachment link.","Not stated.","There is.","Not stated."],"k":"BF contact → secure attachment.","rn":[["","What does breastfeeding contact promote?","What does breastfeeding contact promote?","Secure attachment"]]},{"id":6111,"t":"Influences on Pregnancy","s":"Which statement about maternal weight change in pregnancy is correct?","o":["Excessive and insufficient change are both harmful","Only excessive weight gain is linked to harm","Only insufficient weight gain is linked to harm","Weight change matters only in the third trimester of pregnancy","Weight loss in pregnancy is protective"],"e":"Behavioural influences include <b>maternal weight change – excessive or insufficient</b> – associated with foetal/infant mortality, difficult labour, LBW and infant morbidity.","w":["Both.","Both.","Not stated.","Not stated."],"k":"Excessive or insufficient weight change both harmful."},{"id":6112,"t":"Influences on Pregnancy","s":"According to de Graaf (2013), which outcomes does socioeconomic disadvantage increase the risk of?","o":["Maternal morbidity/mortality, LBW and infant morbidity/mortality","Maternal morbidity/mortality, high birth weight and infant asthma","LBW and infant morbidity, but not maternal morbidity","Unplanned pregnancy, LBW and later ADHD in the child","Baby blues, PND and postnatal PTSD symptoms"],"e":"Socioeconomic disadvantage increases the risk of maternal morbidity and mortality, low birth weight and infant morbidity and mortality (de Graaf 2013). Lower SES is also associated with less breastfeeding.","w":["Low birth weight, not high.","Maternal morbidity/mortality is included.","Unplanned pregnancy and ADHD are linked to other factors.","Those are perinatal mental health conditions, not the de Graaf outcomes."],"k":"NICE abbreviation.","rn":[["","Which infant risks does socioeconomic disadvantage raise?","Which infant risks does socioeconomic disadvantage raise?","Low birth weight; Infant morbidity and mortality"],["","Which maternal risks does socioeconomic disadvantage raise?","Which maternal risks does socioeconomic disadvantage raise?","Maternal morbidity; Maternal mortality"]]},{"id":6113,"t":"Support in Pregnancy and Labour","s":"Which statement summarises why social support matters in pregnancy and childbirth?","o":["More control and satisfaction; less stress, trauma and PND","More perceived control and satisfaction, but more medical intervention","Shorter labour only, with no psychological benefit","Less stress, but more traumatic births","Benefits mainly for fathers rather than mothers"],"e":"Support (Hodnett et al., 2013): greater perceived control, less stress, greater satisfaction, lower likelihood of traumatic birth and PND; continuous labour support also shortens labour, reduces intervention and improves Apgar scores.","w":["Less intervention.","Psychological benefits too.","Less traumatic birth.","For the woman."],"k":"Benefits of social support.","rn":[["","Which 3 benefits of support help during pregnancy and birth?","Which 3 benefits of support help during pregnancy and birth?","Greater perceived control; Less stress; Greater birth satisfaction"]]},{"id":6114,"t":"Attachment","s":"Which pairing of developmental age and attachment milestone is correct?","o":["7 months–2 years: separation anxiety, behaviours to specific people","6 weeks–7 months: separation anxiety, behaviours to specific people","0–6 weeks: separation anxiety, behaviours to specific people","2 years+: separation anxiety, behaviours to specific people","7 months–2 years: non-specific attachment behaviour"],"e":"<b>7 mo–2 yr</b>: clear-cut attachment with separation anxiety. 0–6 wk: non-specific; 6 wk–7 mo: developing preference; 2 yr+: separation anxiety diminishes.","w":["That is a developing preference.","Non-specific behaviour.","Separation anxiety diminishes.","Specific people."],"k":"Match ages to attachment stages.","rn":[["","What are the features of clear-cut attachment?","What are the features of clear-cut attachment?","Behaviour at specific people; Esp. parents; Separation anxiety"],["","When does separation anxiety appear?","When does separation anxiety appear?","Clear-cut attachment stage; 7 months - 2 years"]]},{"id":6115,"t":"Parent-Infant Bonding","s":"Which statement about bonding is <b>not</b> supported?","o":["Bonding is impossible if early contact is limited","Babies associate their mother’s smell with comfort","Bonding and attachment with the father is important","Earlier intervention gives better outcomes","Children have some resilience"],"e":"<b>All is not lost</b> if there is less early contact (incubators, adoption); children have resilience and early intervention helps.","w":["Supported.","Supported.","Supported.","Supported."],"k":"Limited early contact does not make bonding impossible.","rn":[["","Is all lost if there is less early contact?","Is all lost if there is less early contact?","No"]]},{"id":6116,"t":"Emotions in Each Trimester","s":"Which explanation best accounts for why a woman with an unplanned pregnancy, financial worries and little family support might feel more negative in the first trimester?","o":["They shape first-trimester swings in feeling","They matter only after birth, as risk factors for baby blues","These protect against negative feelings by prompting support","First-trimester mood is mainly hormonal, so they matter little","These shape third-trimester mood more than the first"],"e":"First-trimester feelings depend on factors such as <b>planned/unplanned pregnancy</b>, <b>financial situation</b> and <b>family support</b>, as well as ailments, perceived restriction and loss of independence.","w":["First trimester.","Not protective.","They do matter.","Third is discomfort."],"k":"Psychosocial factors shape first-trimester mood.","rn":[["","Third trimester: what happens to emotions?","Third trimester: what happens to emotions?","Negative feelings can come back"],["","Second trimester: what happens to negative feelings?","Second trimester: what happens to negative feelings?","They can lessen"],["","Why do negative feelings lessen in the second trimester?","Why do negative feelings lessen in the second trimester?","Less nausea and vomiting; More adaptation; Some idea about pregnancy care"]]},{"id":6117,"t":"Breastfeeding","s":"Which statement about immunomodulatory qualities of breast milk is correct?","o":["They are a health benefit for the baby (Victora et al., 2016)","They are a health benefit for the mother (Victora et al., 2016)","They explain the link with intelligence (Horta et al., 2015)","They are a confounding factor in breastfeeding studies","They reduce rates of asthma in the mother"],"e":"Health benefits for the baby include the <b>immunomodulatory qualities of breast milk</b>, with lower infectious mortality and lower asthma rates.","w":["Baby.","Not that explanation.","Not confounding.","Lower asthma in the baby."],"k":"Breast milk is immunomodulatory.","rn":[["","What are the health benefits of breastfeeding for the baby?","What are the health benefits of breastfeeding for the baby?","Lower mortality from infectious disease; Lower rates of asthma; Immunomodulatory qualities of breast milk"]]},{"id":6118,"t":"Attachment","s":"A 3-year-old is in the final stage of attachment (reciprocal relationship, 2 years onwards). Which change is characteristic of this stage?","o":["Separation anxiety diminishes","Separation anxiety first appears","A preference for parents first develops","Attachment behaviour is non-specific","Attachment is directed to any adult"],"e":"Reciprocal relationship (2 years onwards): separation anxiety diminishes. Separation anxiety is a feature of clear-cut attachment (7 months–2 years), when attachment behaviours are directed to specific people.","w":["It first appears in clear-cut attachment (7 mo–2 yr).","That is attachment in the making (6 wk–7 mo).","That is pre-attachment (0–6 wk).","Attachment is a tie to specific people."],"k":"MBRRACE-UK listed as a resource.","rn":[["","What are the features of the reciprocal relationship stage?","What are the features of the reciprocal relationship stage?","Separation anxiety diminishes; Better at keeping contact; With preferred people"],["","When does separation anxiety appear?","When does separation anxiety appear?","Clear-cut attachment stage; 7 months - 2 years"]]},{"id":6119,"t":"Baby Blues and Postnatal Depression","s":"On day 5 after a vaginal birth, a mother is tearful and irritable. At 4 weeks her low mood persists, she has lost interest in her baby and she had depression before. What is the most appropriate interpretation?","o":["Possible PND: low mood beyond 2 weeks warrants screening","Baby blues: reassure, as it lasts up to 6 weeks","Baby blues: no action, as it is normal after a vaginal birth","Tokophobia: refer for fear of childbirth","PTSD: all vaginal births cause trauma symptoms"],"e":"Baby blues affects about half of women from day 3–4 and usually stops by day 10 without treatment. If low mood lasts more than 2 weeks, screening for depression is warranted. PND affects 10–15%, onset usually within 1–2 months; previous depression is a risk factor.","w":["Baby blues usually stops by day 10.","Persistence to 4 weeks is not baby blues.","Tokophobia is fear of childbirth, before birth.","PTSD symptoms occur in about a quarter, not all."],"k":"Low mood > 2 weeks → screen for PND.","rn":[["","When should you screen for depression after birth?","When should you screen for depression after birth?","Low mood over 2 weeks"]]},{"id":6120,"t":"Adaptation to Parenthood","s":"A mother asks whether she should feel guilty that she cannot be perfect. Which concepts best support her?","o":["Winnicott’s “good enough mother” and “it takes a village”","The “SuperMom” ideal and “it takes a village”","Winnicott’s “good enough mother” and the biomedical view of birth","Silva and Carneiro’s “accessories” and “it takes a village”","Kim’s adaptation to parenthood and the “SuperMom” ideal"],"e":"Winnicott's <b>“good enough mother”</b> and <b>“it takes a village to raise a child”</b> counter the unrealistic “SuperMom” ideal: meeting the baby's basic needs with support from others is what matters.","w":["SuperMom is the unrealistic ideal.","Not relevant.","About fathers.","SuperMom is unrealistic."],"k":"Good enough mother; village support.","rn":[["","Whose concept is the \"good enough mother\"?","Whose concept is the \"good enough mother\"?","Winnicott (1953)"]]},{"id":6121,"t":"Baby Blues and Postnatal Depression","s":"A 28-year-old woman gave birth to her first baby 4 days ago. Since yesterday she has felt emotional, bursts into tears easily and feels irritable and restless, even though she expected to feel happy. She is eating and caring for her baby. Her midwife plans to review her mood again later.<br><br>What is the most likely explanation, and when would screening for depression be warranted?","o":["Baby blues; screen if low mood lasts more than 2 weeks","Postnatal depression; screen immediately, as onset is usually day 3–4","Baby blues; screen only if symptoms continue beyond 6 months, when the blues normally end","Tokophobia; screen if fear of childbirth continues","PTSD; screen if symptoms continue after the first week"],"e":"Step 1: baby blues affect about half of women within the first week, usually from day 3 or 4, with feeling emotional, tearfulness, irritability, low mood, anxiety and restlessness; it usually stops by day 10 and needs no treatment. Step 2: if low mood lasts more than 2 weeks, screening for depression is warranted, because postnatal depression (PND) is a different condition with onset usually within 1–2 months.","w":["PND onset is usually within 1–2 months; day 3–4 fits baby blues.","Baby blues usually stops by day 10, so 6 months is far too late.","Tokophobia is an extreme fear of childbirth, which she does not describe.","Her symptoms fit baby blues, not post-traumatic stress disorder."],"k":"Baby blues: day 3–4 to day 10; low mood >2 weeks → screen for depression.","rn":[["","When should you screen for depression after birth?","When should you screen for depression after birth?","Low mood over 2 weeks"],["","What are 2 mood symptoms of baby blues?","What are 2 mood symptoms of baby blues?","Emotional, bursting into tears; Low mood"],["","Baby blues","Baby blues","Mood changes; In first week after childbirth"]]},{"id":6122,"t":"Baby Blues and Postnatal Depression","s":"A 32-year-old woman arrived in the UK as an asylum seeker a year ago and is now 6 weeks post partum. She had depression in her early twenties and felt anxious during this pregnancy. She has little contact with family and her partner recently lost his job. Her health visitor considers her mental health risk.<br><br>Which condition is she at particular risk of, and which of her features are listed risk factors?","o":["PND; previous mental illness, anxiety in pregnancy, poor support, a stressful event and refugee status","Baby blues; poor support and refugee status only","Tokophobia; previous depression and anxiety in pregnancy","PND; only her previous depression, as social factors do not add risk","PTSD; refugee status and a job loss, as these are the only listed risk factors for perinatal anxiety disorders"],"e":"Step 1: postnatal depression (PND) affects 10–15% of mothers, with onset usually within 1–2 months of birth, which fits her timing. Step 2: listed PND risk factors (Howard 2014) include previous mental health problems, depression or anxiety in pregnancy, poor support, a recent stressful event such as job loss, domestic violence or abuse, and arriving in a developed country as a refugee or asylum seeker; she has most of these.","w":["Baby blues starts in the first week and is not linked to these risks.","Tokophobia is a fear of childbirth, not a postnatal mood disorder.","Social factors such as poor support and refugee status are listed PND risks.","These are PND risk factors, not a listed PTSD risk list."],"k":"PND risks: past mental illness, antenatal anxiety/depression, poor support, stress, refugee status.","rn":[["","Which 2 mental health histories raise PND risk?","Which 2 mental health histories raise PND risk?","Previous mental health problems; Depression/anxiety in pregnancy"],["","Which 2 other risk factors raise PND risk?","Which 2 other risk factors raise PND risk?","Recent stressful event; Refugee or asylum seeker"],["","What is a strong predictor of postnatal mental illness?","What is a strong predictor of postnatal mental illness?","Poor mental health in pregnancy"]]},{"id":6123,"t":"Mental Health in Pregnancy","s":"A 26-year-old woman becomes depressed in her second trimester but does not seek help, believing that pregnancy protects against mental illness. She also starts smoking again. Her midwife discovers this at 30 weeks and explains the possible consequences of untreated depression for her baby.<br><br>Which consequences fit the lecture, and is her belief correct?","o":["Low birth weight, preterm delivery and later child ADHD; her belief is wrong","Higher birth weight and longer pregnancy; her belief is correct","No effect on the baby, as only postnatal depression matters; her belief is correct","Only baby blues after birth, which needs no treatment; her belief is partly correct, as pregnancy reduces mental illness","Better infant attachment; her belief is wrong"],"e":"Step 1: untreated depression in pregnancy is associated with low birth weight, increased risk of preterm delivery and adverse child outcomes (emotional problems, ADHD, conduct disorder), and women with mental illness are more likely to misuse nicotine and alcohol. Step 2: pregnancy is not protective against mental illness, and poor mental health in pregnancy is a strong predictor of postnatal illness, so her belief is wrong.","w":["Untreated depression is linked to low birth weight and preterm delivery.","Antenatal depression has foetal and child consequences, and pregnancy is not protective.","Antenatal illness predicts postnatal illness, not just baby blues.","Depression is linked to attachment problems, not better attachment."],"k":"Pregnancy is not protective; antenatal depression → LBW, preterm, child ADHD.","rn":[["","Untreated depression in pregnancy: risks to the baby?","Untreated depression in pregnancy: risks to the baby?","Low birth weight; Pre-term delivery"]]},{"id":6124,"t":"Influences on Pregnancy","s":"A 30-year-old pregnant teacher has a very stressful term at work. She notices her blood pressure is higher, and her doctor explains that her stress hormones may affect the blood supply to the placenta. She has also started skipping meals and drinking more coffee and wine in the evening.<br><br>Which pathways link her stress to the foetus?","o":["A direct physiological pathway (hormones, BP, placenta) and an indirect behavioural pathway","Only an indirect pathway, as stress cannot affect the foetus physiologically","Only a direct pathway, as behaviour does not affect the foetus","A direct pathway through her behaviour and an indirect pathway through hormones and placental blood flow","Neither, as stress affects only the mother"],"e":"Step 1: stress has a direct physiological response in the mother (hormonal changes, blood pressure) that can affect the foetus via the placental blood supply. Step 2: stress also has an indirect psychological route through changes in the mother's behaviour, such as poor nutrition and alcohol use, which are behavioural influences linked to adverse foetal outcomes.","w":["Stress has a direct physiological pathway too.","Behaviour change is the indirect pathway, which also affects the foetus.","The labels are reversed: hormones are direct, behaviour is indirect.","Both pathways can affect the foetus."],"k":"Stress → foetus: direct (hormones, BP, placenta) + indirect (behaviour).","rn":[["","What does the direct physiological stress route involve?","What does the direct physiological stress route involve?","Hormones; BP (blood pressure); Placental blood supply"],["","What are the 2 routes by which stress affects the foetus?","What are the 2 routes by which stress affects the foetus?","Direct physiological; Indirect psychological"]]},{"id":6125,"t":"Emotions in Each Trimester","s":"A 24-year-old woman with an unplanned pregnancy has severe nausea, reflux and insomnia in her first weeks and feels a loss of independence. By the middle of her pregnancy she feels much better, has adapted to the changes and has been told about her pregnancy care. In the final weeks she has backache, pelvic girdle pain and exhaustion and feels low again.<br><br>Which statement best describes her pattern?","o":["Negative feelings lessen in the second trimester and may return in the third","Mood is most negative in the second trimester, when mental health problems peak","Mood is stable through pregnancy and changes only after birth","The first trimester is always positive, because excitement about the pregnancy outweighs all of the early ailments","Mood improves steadily in every trimester to birth"],"e":"Step 1: in the first trimester mood fluctuates depending on ailments (nausea, reflux, insomnia), whether the pregnancy is planned, money, support, perceived restriction and loss of independence; in the second trimester negative feelings may lessen with reduced nausea, adaptation and information about care. Step 2: in the third trimester negative feelings may come back because of discomfort (pelvic girdle pain, backache), insomnia and exhaustion; mental health problems are least common in the second trimester.","w":["Mental health problems are least common in the second trimester.","The psychological state is dynamic in every trimester.","First-trimester feelings fluctuate and depend on ailments and circumstances.","Negative feelings may return in the third trimester."],"k":"Second trimester: fewest problems; third: discomfort brings negativity back.","rn":[["","Why do negative feelings return in the third trimester?","Why do negative feelings return in the third trimester?","Increasing discomfort; Insomnia; Tiredness / exhaustion"],["","Why do negative feelings lessen in the second trimester?","Why do negative feelings lessen in the second trimester?","Less nausea and vomiting; More adaptation; Some idea about pregnancy care"],["","Second trimester: what happens to negative feelings?","Second trimester: what happens to negative feelings?","They can lessen"]]},{"id":6126,"t":"Support in Pregnancy and Labour","s":"A hospital introduces a scheme in which every woman has a trained supporter with her continuously throughout labour. A year later, midwives audit the results compared with the previous year. They want to know which outcomes the lecture's evidence would predict to improve specifically from continuous support during labour.<br><br>Which outcomes should they expect?","o":["Shorter labour, less medical or surgical intervention and better Apgar scores","Higher rates of tokophobia and more caesarean sections","Longer labour but greater satisfaction with birth","Lower breastfeeding rates and more baby blues","Lower birth weight, because labour is shorter and babies are delivered sooner than they would be without support"],"e":"Step 1: support in pregnancy and birth (Hodnett et al., 2013) gives greater perceived control, less stress, greater satisfaction and lower likelihood of traumatic birth and PND. Step 2: continuous support in labour specifically is associated with shorter labour, less medical/surgical intervention and better Apgar scores.","w":["Support reduces intervention and is not linked to more fear of childbirth.","Continuous support shortens labour.","These are not outcomes the lecture links to support.","Low birth weight is not an outcome of continuous labour support."],"k":"Continuous labour support: shorter labour, less intervention, better Apgar.","rn":[["","What are the benefits of continuous support in labour?","What are the benefits of continuous support in labour?","Shorter labour; Less medical / surgical intervention; Better Apgar scores"]]},{"id":6127,"t":"Attachment","s":"A health visitor sees three babies in one clinic. Baby A, aged 3 weeks, settles with anyone who holds her. Baby B, aged 4 months, prefers the comfort of his parents but does not cry when they leave. Baby C, aged 11 months, cries and protests when her mother leaves the room.<br><br>Which attachment stages are these babies in?","o":["A: pre-attachment; B: attachment in the making; C: clear-cut attachment","A: attachment in the making; B: clear-cut; C: reciprocal relationship","A: pre-attachment; B: clear-cut; C: reciprocal relationship, as separation anxiety starts at this age","A: clear-cut; B: pre-attachment; C: attachment in the making","All three are in pre-attachment until 2 years"],"e":"Step 1: pre-attachment (0–6 weeks) shows non-specific behaviour (baby A), and attachment in the making (6 weeks–7 months) shows a developing preference for certain people without separation anxiety (baby B). Step 2: clear-cut attachment (7 months–2 years) has behaviours directed to specific people and separation anxiety (baby C); in the reciprocal stage (from 2 years) separation anxiety diminishes.","w":["Baby A is under 6 weeks and non-specific, so pre-attachment.","Baby B has no separation anxiety yet, and separation anxiety diminishes in the reciprocal stage.","The stages are in the wrong order.","The stages change at 6 weeks, 7 months and 2 years."],"k":"0–6 wk pre; 6 wk–7 mo in the making; 7 mo–2 yr clear-cut (separation anxiety).","rn":[["","When does separation anxiety appear?","When does separation anxiety appear?","Clear-cut attachment stage; 7 months - 2 years"],["","What are the features of clear-cut attachment?","What are the features of clear-cut attachment?","Behaviour at specific people; Esp. parents; Separation anxiety"],["","What ages are pre-attachment and attachment in the making?","What ages are pre-attachment and attachment in the making?","Pre-attachment: 0-6 weeks; Making: 6 weeks-7 months"]]},{"id":6128,"t":"Baby Blues and Postnatal Depression","s":"A 35-year-old mother has untreated postnatal depression for most of her baby's first year. Her health visitor notices that she rarely responds to her baby's emotions, and the baby seems less responsive to faces and voices. At 18 months, the child's learning and cognitive development are behind.<br><br>Which pathway best explains the child's delay?","o":["Poorer mother–baby interaction and attachment problems lead to poorer learning","Depression passes directly into breast milk and slows brain growth","Baby blues continuing past day 10 lead to delay","Lack of continuous support in labour leads to delay","The child has tokophobia, which delays learning because of fear of the parent leaving the room"],"e":"Step 1: an important pathway from PND to child outcomes is mother–baby interaction (Murray et al. 2014): depressed mothers are less sensitive to the child's emotions, and infants are less responsive to faces and voices. Step 2: problems with psychological attachment are then more likely, which leads to poorer infant learning and cognitive development.","w":["The lecture does not describe depression passing into breast milk.","Baby blues stops by day 10; this is PND.","Labour support affects labour outcomes, not this pathway.","Tokophobia is a maternal fear of childbirth."],"k":"PND → poor interaction/attachment → poorer infant learning.","rn":[["","How are infants of mothers with PND affected?","How are infants of mothers with PND affected?","Less responsive to faces, voices; Attachment problems more likely"],["","How does PND affect child development?","How does PND affect child development?","Via mother-baby interaction"],["","How does postnatal depression affect the infant?","How does postnatal depression affect the infant?","Poor engagement with infant; Poorer infant learning; Poorer cognitive development"]]},{"id":6129,"t":"Breastfeeding","s":"A study finds that breastfed children score higher on intelligence tests in childhood and adulthood, and the effect is stronger in disadvantaged children. A sceptical student points out that breastfeeding mothers in the study had higher education and income, were more likely to have a partner and were less likely to smoke.<br><br>Which explanation is the student offering, and which other explanations does the lecture give?","o":["Confounding factors; plus nutrients in milk and contact during feeding","Nutrients in breast milk; plus lactational amenorrhoea","Secure attachment; plus lower infectious mortality","Confounding factors; plus lower rates of breast and ovarian cancer in the mothers who breastfeed for longer","Epigenetics; plus kangaroo care"],"e":"Step 1: the student describes the third explanation: other factors linked to both breastfeeding and cognitive development, such as higher education and SES, having a partner and not smoking (confounders). Step 2: the other two explanations are (1) nutrients in breast milk (microbiome, immune responses, prebiotics) and (2) physical and psychological contact during feeding, e.g. via secure attachment.","w":["Lactational amenorrhoea is a maternal benefit (birth spacing), not an explanation of IQ.","The student's point is about confounders, and infection rates are a health benefit.","Maternal cancer benefits do not explain the child IQ link.","These are not the lecture's explanations of the BF–IQ link."],"k":"BF–IQ: nutrients, contact/attachment, confounders (SES, education).","rn":[["","What are the 3 explanations for breastfeeding's cognitive benefit?","What are the 3 explanations for breastfeeding's cognitive benefit?","Nutrients in breast milk; Physical and psychological contact; Other linked factors"]]},{"id":6130,"t":"Parent-Infant Bonding","s":"A baby born at 30 weeks spends several weeks in an incubator and has little close contact with her parents at first. Her mother worries that bonding is now impossible. The neonatal nurse encourages skin-to-skin contact and explains how babies normally bond through the senses.<br><br>What is the best response to the mother's worry?","o":["All is not lost: kangaroo care helps, children are resilient and earlier intervention is better","Bonding is impossible after missed early contact","Bonding happens only through feeding, so contact does not matter","She should wait until the reciprocal stage at 2 years before trying","Only the father can now bond with her, because the mother missed the first weeks of contact"],"e":"Step 1: babies normally bond through physical contact (touch, warmth, slings), smell (mother's pheromones linked to comfort and food), sight (3-day-olds recognise mother) and sound (preference for mother's voice). Step 2: all is not lost with less early contact, as in incubators (advantages of kangaroo care) or adoption; children have some resilience, and outcomes are better with earlier intervention.","w":["The lecture says all is not lost with reduced early contact.","Bonding routes include contact, smell, sight and sound.","Earlier intervention gives better outcomes, so waiting is wrong.","Bonding with both parents matters, and the mother can still bond."],"k":"Less early contact can be offset: kangaroo care, resilience, early intervention.","rn":[["","What does Rutter (2008) say about children?","What does Rutter (2008) say about children?","Some resilience; Better with earlier intervention"],["","What are examples of less early contact?","What are examples of less early contact?","Incubator babies (kangaroo care); Adopted children"],["","What helps incubator babies bond?","What helps incubator babies bond?","Kangaroo care (Kambarami 1998)"]]}]);
